WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:07.053
- Welcome everybody, can I
call the board into session?

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.300
Can I first of all start
with apologies for absence.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.600
So we are missing today Rakesh Kapoor,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.910
Munir Pirmohamed, Steve Powis,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.710
and Susan Kilsby who send apologies.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.310
Susan was with us this morning

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:23.943
in the private board session.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:28.260
Any declarations of
interest from directors

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.653
since the last meeting
that have arisen on you?

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.893
Thank you.

00:00:34.995 --> 00:00:36.750
And can I ask for the board's
approval for the minutes

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.423
of the board meeting held on 18th of May?

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.030
Thank you very much.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.970
Well, let me just turn if we might

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:48.090
to just to an update from me.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.819
First of all actually,

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:53.580
we're delighted that we
are in Birmingham today

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.080
holding our board meeting.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.540
And actually there are a series
of visits tomorrow morning

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.030
where the board will
separate into small groups

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.518
and go out with different executives

00:01:02.518 --> 00:01:06.300
into I think four different areas.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.540
So one of the groups will
go and look at primary care

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.580
and community integration.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.040
A second group will be
looking at acute services,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.320
going to one of our providers

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.590
and acute services including
emergency services.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.940
Separately, we have looking
at children's health,

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.771
children's services, including
actually social care.

00:01:27.771 --> 00:01:28.604
And the interactions are very important

00:01:28.604 --> 00:01:31.590
as we think about the
Integrated Care Board

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.120
and Integrated Care Systems.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.892
And then the fourth group,

00:01:34.892 --> 00:01:35.970
we're looking at specialist services,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.610
particularly looking at women's health

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.700
and also separately at
mental health services.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.640
So I think really very good
visits that are going to be made

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.610
by the board to go and see

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.000
in the front line how
these various aspects

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.200
are operating here in the ICB.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.240
Now, I'd also just like
to thank our regional

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.410
and ICB colleagues

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.963
who clearly have put a lot
of work into hosting us.

00:02:00.963 --> 00:02:04.380
It's not easy when I suppose
the board comes into town,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.680
but Dale Bywater as the regional
director of the Midlands

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.060
has done a huge amount and
his deputy Rebecca Farmer

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.455
who actually be at the
board dinner tonight,

00:02:14.455 --> 00:02:17.280
has also clearly been instrumental

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.130
in arranging this meeting,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.300
but also actually more importantly,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.630
the visits that are occurring tomorrow.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.496
And clearly the overall
purpose of being here

00:02:27.496 --> 00:02:30.030
is actually to focus on the ICB working

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.980
and system collaboration

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.500
and to get a real feel for that.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.020
I've referenced the dinner
that will be held tonight,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.320
and we have colleagues
coming from across the system

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.870
and from local authorities
and from community

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.340
to join the board.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.060
It's roughly the same number
of guests as board members,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.200
so it should be a very good

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.143
and interactive conversation over dinner.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.180
Moving on from the logistics,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.020
I do just want to take a moment to note

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.705
that two of our colleagues,
this for two of our colleagues,

00:03:05.705 --> 00:03:10.110
this is their last meeting
as directors of NHS England.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.270
So Tim Ferris, Professor Tim Ferris,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.613
who's going to be leaving us in September,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.900
was a non-executive director on the board

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.450
of NHS Improvement for three years,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:26.010
I think before being suborned
into an executive role,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.770
essentially as a national
director for transformation,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.930
which he started actually
I think in mid May,

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.320
10th of May, 2021, and Tim,

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.270
our thanks to you and our appreciation

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.910
to you for an absolutely outstanding job,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.520
and clearly we hope to
maintain very good contact

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.130
and interaction and
relatively close working

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.180
with you as we go into the
future, but we'll miss you.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.680
And then Sir David Sloman,

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.860
Sir David also leaving in September.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.360
We've made a joke
slightly earlier, I mean,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.980
are you retiring or not retiring?

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.520
It depends on what day we ask you.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.010
But actually you joined us
as Chief Operating Officer

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.140
on the 14th of December 21.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.600
You'd previously been the
London Regional Director

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.650
and through COVID, which
was a very major pressure

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.010
on role and and you made an
outstanding contribution there

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.600
and you've continued to make

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.090
that outstanding contribution as our COO.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.950
So thank you and we will
miss you and I'm sure Amanda

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.330
will expand on those words for me

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.640
or certainly add her thanks

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.440
for the executives and
I'm speaking on behalf

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.398
of I suppose execs,

00:04:30.398 --> 00:04:32.365
but also predominantly the non-execs here.

00:04:32.365 --> 00:04:34.523
So thank you very much Tim,
thank you very much David.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.140
Just some highlights,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:41.730
I think since we last met
in public as the board,

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.320
clearly the long-term workforce plan

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.390
was published on the 30th of June,

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.360
received broadly positive support

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.610
and a very good actually media response.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:54.493
So actually to Chris and
the and the comms team,

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:55.808
thank you very much for that engagement.

00:04:55.808 --> 00:04:58.320
It was probably made easy by the fact

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.230
it's a very high quality piece of work.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.330
It really is actually.

00:05:04.153 --> 00:05:05.880
And it was a long time in the making

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.620
and it was really good to get the support,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.200
such clear support from government on it.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.030
And I think it makes a real difference.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:16.020
It's a necessary, it's a seminal
building block effectively

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.660
for the future of the NHS.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:22.680
And next of course is
implementation and delivery.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.050
And clearly we've now turned our focus

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.470
very much to that and probably eating

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:29.970
into a lot of what
Amanda was going to say,

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.920
then she'll have to sort
of move her feet quite fast

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.537
when I finish then,

00:05:33.537 --> 00:05:36.540
because I was going to say
the 75th birthday of the NHS,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.410
which also celebrated and
rightly celebrated in July

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.690
and was marked by a series of events.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.071
And many of the board joined us all

00:05:45.071 --> 00:05:47.580
in the Westminster Abbey service

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.070
and also the Parliamentary
awards and a range

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.070
of other celebratory events.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.076
And now we're looking forward clearly

00:05:55.076 --> 00:05:56.719
to the next 75 years.

00:05:56.719 --> 00:06:00.390
I'm not going to talk
about industrial action,

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.740
clearly it's having a real impact,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.653
but I'll leave that if I might.

00:06:03.653 --> 00:06:06.153
To Amanda or to David.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.320
We have talked as a
board about the challenge

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.610
of the absorption,

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.960
essentially the merger
with HEE and NHS digital.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.160
So the board now clearly is
overseeing a much broader agenda

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.790
of items and those, those NHS Digital

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.370
and HEE were of themselves
substantive boards

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.620
covering substantive agendas,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.900
which have now essentially been absorbed.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.460
We've managed that through transition

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.170
effectively through a committee structure,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.380
which we know whether
it's the data and digital

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.430
and technology committee or actually

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.720
the workforce and talent,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.140
the Workforce Training
and Education Committee.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.480
And we've been delighted that three

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.920
of the non-executive directors

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.860
from HEE and three of the
non-executive directors

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.400
from NHS Digital have joined
those committees effectively

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.200
as associate directors.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.680
And it allows us to maintain
a runway of oversight

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.330
over the issues that those boards

00:07:04.182 --> 00:07:07.860
were in any event
responsible for overseeing.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.100
And it provides continuity and frankly,

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.770
their very valuable expertise

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.110
and we have asked them if they will extend

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.320
their terms of office
and it's highly likely

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.640
that they will do that.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.403
And that I think is really very good.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.190
Amanda and I and Tim
and other colleagues met

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.261
with the Genomics England board recently

00:07:32.261 --> 00:07:33.720
as part of their 10th anniversary.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.138
As you know, they are part,

00:07:36.138 --> 00:07:38.940
as well as many things,

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:42.390
they run whole genome sequencing
and they run oversight

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.540
of the library, the genetic library.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.610
And we celebrated with them
their 10 year anniversary

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.753
and then also had a conversation with them

00:07:49.753 --> 00:07:50.640
about actually the interaction

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.130
of Genomics England with actually

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:56.421
the very substantial word
that NHSS England does

00:07:56.421 --> 00:07:58.020
in its own genomics organisation.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.880
I mean, effectively when panel testing

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.178
and individual gene testing,

00:08:02.178 --> 00:08:03.200
actually NHS England probably does 90%

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.003
of the genetic testing
that's actually carried out

00:08:07.003 --> 00:08:08.490
by the NHS in England.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:11.250
Clearly leads on whole genome sequencing

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.020
and engages very, very significantly

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.014
and is at the forefront I think

00:08:15.014 --> 00:08:17.823
of that evolution or that development.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:23.010
I just want to reference
that we have set up

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.280
a primary care subgroup of the board.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.010
It's a mix of executives
and non-executives

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:32.220
and we are really looking
at a range of things,

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.370
predominantly beyond the next contract,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.530
just the current contact discussions

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.170
or the next contract discussions
to think about the future

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:42.210
of primary care.

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.010
And that subgroup will
meet four more times,

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.983
I think between now and
the end of the year.

00:08:46.983 --> 00:08:48.090
But as importantly or more importantly,

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:51.480
we'll do a series of
stakeholder round tables,

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.243
both to hear the views of people,

00:08:54.243 --> 00:08:58.740
who have really real expertise,
strong views in this space,

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.013
but also to communicate out

00:09:00.013 --> 00:09:04.006
the way that our thinking might develop.

00:09:04.006 --> 00:09:06.870
And as we think about looking
at primary care reform,

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.930
what it might look like in
five years or seven years

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.000
or 10 years, and then
working back from that,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.070
what might be the steps
and what might we imagine

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.760
and envision would be the model

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.310
for primary care in no
way that it is compulsory,

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.230
but actually in terms
of alternative models

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.960
that might be offered for our very able

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.780
and very committed
primary care practitioners

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.543
to think about engaging with.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:34.923
There are some priorities
for the coming months.

00:09:35.837 --> 00:09:40.837
We have actually on the
agenda an item really to note,

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:45.540
which is the winter plan and
I think it's today Amanda

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:49.440
was sending out a letter
around winter planning

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.040
and Amanda, I'm sure we'll
talk a little more about that.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.251
And then separately, the
New Hospitals Programme

00:09:55.251 --> 00:09:57.000
where in our earlier board meeting

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.980
this morning, the private board meeting,

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:01.320
actually the board has agreed actually

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.170
at the request of the department actually

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.140
that we'll now take the
lead and get engaged

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.110
in delivery of the new
hospitals programme.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.151
So we will take the delivery risk

00:10:12.151 --> 00:10:15.450
and we've agreed that
we'll now oversee that.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.630
But clearly actually oversight
of strategy and engagement

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.850
with the superstructure
of government above us

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:22.920
does remain part of that programme.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.313
But we will lead
effectively on the delivery,

00:10:27.506 --> 00:10:31.353
if I just reflect slightly more.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.803
Okay.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:37.320
If you'll humour me just with,

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.120
I'd just like to talk about a couple

00:10:40.243 --> 00:10:44.610
of the visits I've made and
I'm always very privileged

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:49.080
to do these, so I have a
number of ambulance visits.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.930
So I visited the South East
Coast Ambulance Service

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:54.630
about four or five weeks
ago, which was in Crawley.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:59.325
And that was really again,
gave me huge insights

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:00.858
actually into the pressure
on the ambulance service

00:11:00.858 --> 00:11:02.174
and the way they engage

00:11:02.174 --> 00:11:03.007
with all sorts of different pressures

00:11:03.007 --> 00:11:06.720
and different types of
demand coming at them.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.760
And actually on Tuesday night this week,

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:12.345
I did a shift with the West
Midlands Ambulance Service

00:11:12.345 --> 00:11:15.540
and actually you see the levels of stress

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.860
actually candidly in society

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:23.040
and the demand from
our citizens in society

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.560
and what they asked for
the ambulance service for.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.530
And I would say to you that
mental health conditions

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:30.540
were a very clear feature

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:35.370
of the people that the ambulance

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:39.420
was called out to see.
Visit to Surrey Heartlands

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:43.158
with the minister Neil
O'Brien, with Claire Fuller.

00:11:43.158 --> 00:11:44.480
And we were also looking

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:46.740
particularly at how in
that case Surrey Heartlands

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.710
engages with the older people,

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:52.620
the older cohort and a
separate visit to the Chelsea

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:55.149
and Westminster Hospital
actually with Nick Markham,

00:11:55.149 --> 00:11:56.740
Lord Markham, when that
was to look at foundry,

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:59.310
the systems and the data in operation,

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.010
how much it helped our
clinicians essentially

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:05.463
in their navigation of their task.

00:12:06.336 --> 00:12:09.820
And then this week actually
I'd sort of come back

00:12:10.656 --> 00:12:12.756
to home, born in Wolverhampton.

00:12:12.756 --> 00:12:13.770
So I came back into
Wolverhampton on Monday

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.570
but also to Warsaw and Willenhall

00:12:16.474 --> 00:12:17.776
and Dudley and Sandwell

00:12:17.776 --> 00:12:19.770
and visited a number of
primary care practises.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.870
Had a very good meeting
with the Black Country

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:23.961
primary care collaborative,

00:12:23.961 --> 00:12:26.430
a brilliant group of GPs
and a very interesting

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.560
organisation structure engaging themselves

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.070
with the secondary trust in Wolverhampton.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.870
And then other primary care practises,

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.880
one of which actually was nine practises,

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.880
essentially vertically
owned by New Cross Hospital

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:43.590
but then other practises

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.783
which are are wholly independent of it.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.233
And then there'd be a series of meetings

00:12:49.233 --> 00:12:50.790
with the British Red Cross
and with Michael Marmot,

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:52.065
which was excellent.

00:12:52.065 --> 00:12:54.035
And the Association of
Medical Royal Colleges

00:12:54.035 --> 00:12:55.980
and with the Queen's Nursing Institute,

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.730
which was a very, very good meeting

00:12:58.620 --> 00:12:59.880
about their expectations.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.075
Then also with Camilla Hawthorne,

00:13:02.075 --> 00:13:03.600
chair of the Royal College
of General Practitioners

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.718
and so on.

00:13:04.718 --> 00:13:06.218
There've been a range of
meetings and it's been

00:13:06.218 --> 00:13:08.733
a busy but truly interesting time.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:13.440
So I thought I would share
that with you and having,

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:15.930
we'll now see what I've left
for Amanda to talk about

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.910
that I haven't covered but Amanda,

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:21.483
can I turn to you and ask you to give.

00:13:22.804 --> 00:13:25.683
Yeah, can I ask you to
give your CEO update?

00:13:26.652 --> 00:13:28.020
- Of course, Richard,
thank you very much indeed.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:31.810
So just a number of things
that I want to cover,

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.024
some of which will echo
bits of what Richard

00:13:34.024 --> 00:13:35.781
has already mentioned, but actually

00:13:35.781 --> 00:13:36.630
I'd like to start if I may,

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:40.230
by just saying a word
about the industrial action

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:44.040
which is currently, well still going on

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.330
within the health service.

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:48.330
So since the last time the board met,

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:51.810
we've had a total of eight days
of action by junior doctors

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:54.450
for the first time we
had two days of action

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.910
by consultants and two days of action

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:00.090
from the radiographers earlier this week.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.460
David I know is going to
pick up a bit more later

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.530
on the impact of the action,

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.350
but it is worth noting
that we've now had hundreds

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:10.920
of thousands of patients who've
had appointments postponed

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.560
and we also know large
number of appointments

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:14.670
haven't been made

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:17.820
because people have steered
away from making appointments,

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:20.910
knowing that there was
likely to be disruption.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.702
And we've also had hundreds
of thousands of colleagues

00:14:22.702 --> 00:14:26.010
across the NHS who have had
to work in different ways,

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:26.970
who've had to work harder,

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:31.410
who've had to work together
to manage strike action.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.080
The government has accepted, as you know,

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.340
the recommendations from the DDRB

00:14:37.317 --> 00:14:39.300
and SSRB for this year.

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:41.760
So we are in the process
of supporting employers

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:43.350
to implement that.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.920
Those elements were
above the 3% assumption

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:48.540
that we had been funded for

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.550
but a way through has now been agreed

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:53.400
with the Department of Health
and Social Care and Julian,

00:14:54.411 --> 00:14:56.280
I think we're going to describe
that later in your update.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.200
So I think we're all very conscious

00:14:58.200 --> 00:14:59.790
that all of these conversations

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:03.490
take place with that
backdrop and certainly,

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:07.200
I'd like to thank all of my
colleagues across the country

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.480
for the work that they are
doing to try and minimise

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.850
the impact that it is unfortunately

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:15.030
and inevitably now having at some volume

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:19.110
on our patients. Long-term workforce plan,

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:22.380
you've already mentioned
great piece of work

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:27.380
took not just Navina but
a huge team of people

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:28.600
to pull together

00:15:29.462 --> 00:15:31.514
something that was as
comprehensive as it was

00:15:31.514 --> 00:15:33.870
and actually feels even more important

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:36.240
given the context that I've just described

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:39.630
and not least because I
think it is a real symbol

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:42.930
of hope for the future
and not just for people

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:44.340
who will hopefully join our workforce

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.480
over the next 15 years,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.090
but for those colleagues
who are working here now

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:50.310
who can now see a clear path ahead

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:54.510
where we will have more colleagues trained

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:57.570
to be able to provide the
care that our patients need.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:59.940
So we'll discuss it again a bit later,

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:01.770
but a huge moment for the NHS

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:03.510
and the reception that it received.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:05.060
You've already said it Richard,

00:16:05.996 --> 00:16:08.250
from staff and patient
groups, was really positive.

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:13.020
So a lot of work to do now to
turn that into a delivery plan

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.450
and implementation over
the next few years.

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:16.920
And as we said in the plan,

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:18.810
of course it isn't fixed in stone,

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:22.170
it will be subject to
regular update and review

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.270
and certainly I know the way that Navina

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:25.890
wants to approach implementation

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.290
is the same way as you approach
development of the plan,

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:31.830
which is very much in
partnership with our colleagues,

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:34.620
stakeholders and partners.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.450
You mentioned the NHS 75th,

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:38.610
that's happened since we last met together

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:41.522
as a board and that was again,

00:16:41.522 --> 00:16:43.500
a great event to look
back on what is a period

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:46.110
of just extraordinary change for the NHS

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:47.820
over 75 years, so different now

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:51.750
to where it was 75 years
ago, but the principles,

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:54.390
the founding principles
absolutely rock solid

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.850
and we know how important
they are to the country

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:58.530
as well as they are to our staff.

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:01.440
So I just wanted to put
on record again my thanks

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:06.440
to colleagues in the NHS
assembly who led engagement work

00:17:07.049 --> 00:17:09.390
actually very deep but
very rapid engagement work

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.340
which has allowed colleagues
across the country

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.170
to take a moment to think

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.260
about the longer term
opportunities and goals

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.750
for improving health
and care for communities

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:22.020
and the report that they
produced, hugely valuable

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.230
and I think it will be really
helpful to local leaders

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.930
as well as to us as we
develop our forward plans,

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:31.320
both kind of short term and longer term.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:35.100
So you've mentioned again,
today is an important day

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:36.990
for us around winter planning,

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.400
so just thinking shorter term.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:41.610
So against that backdrop
of industrial action

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.250
and delivery of all of
our recovery priorities,

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:46.710
we have been working
over the last few weeks

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:51.030
to develop the annual
winter preparedness letter

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.320
that we send out to the NHS.

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:54.878
My thanks to Sarah Jane in particular

00:17:54.878 --> 00:17:57.097
for the work that she has
done and to Julian on that,

00:17:57.097 --> 00:18:02.097
it is the earliest that we
have ever put this together

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.600
and been able to share it with the NHS

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.670
and that's partly because
of course it builds

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:11.190
on all the work that went
on in pulling together

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:14.070
and publishing the Urgent
Emergency Care Recovery Plan

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:16.650
earlier this year back in January.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.800
So we've already seen now months of work

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:22.170
to put the actions that were described

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:24.360
in the Urgent Emergency
Care Recovery plan in place

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.100
and we've seen that begin to bear fruit.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.290
That's one of the things
we've described in the letter

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.300
is it's not only that we have seen

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:31.470
improved performance

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:34.027
since the real peak of pressure in winter.

00:18:34.027 --> 00:18:36.540
Yeah it's also improved performance

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.640
compared with this time last year.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.370
So it gives us a kind of
moment to moment comparison

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:42.600
and we don't think we can see

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.490
some of the things that happened

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:45.330
over winter and some of the
things that have happened

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:47.850
subsequently have begun
to build that platform

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.790
from which we are now hoping
to build over the summer

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:54.240
so that we go into this winter in an even,

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.400
well in a more resilient position.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.790
So we'll talk about
that much more in detail

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:01.410
but like last year is worth saying

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.080
that we will be doing much more engagement

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:07.620
with trust leaders, with
ICB leaders to make sure

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:11.160
that we've worked jointly,
both on implementation

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.540
of the stuff that's going out today,

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:14.490
but also to ask ourselves constantly

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:17.370
whether there is more that
we could be doing together

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:19.260
to make sure that we
are supporting patients

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:22.440
as effectively as possible
going into winter.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:25.110
Couple of other things I
wanted to mention, again,

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:29.070
just sort of looking to
to the sort of short term,

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:33.900
medium term 2024, 25 planning.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:36.600
Again sounds slightly odd
to be talking about that

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:38.174
at this point in the year,

00:19:38.174 --> 00:19:39.690
but we are really keen to
take a similar approach

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:42.030
so to do early engagement,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.760
to give people the maximum amount of time

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:47.370
then to work with us
on plans for next year

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.941
but also to get ahead in
thinking about implementation.

00:19:49.941 --> 00:19:51.930
We do know financially

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.030
it is going to be another challenging year

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:55.920
as we look ahead to next year, again,

00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:58.140
Julie might want to say a
bit more about that later.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:00.000
So it's the other thing in our minds

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.710
about giving people plenty of time

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:04.410
to really work with us
and to think about this.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:06.660
So over the coming months
we're going to be engaging

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.210
with regions and systems on the priorities

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:11.670
for the NHS and how we'll achieve them

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:14.880
and once again we do want
to give maximum flexibility

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.380
for local solutions

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:19.860
in line with our national
operating framework.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:21.686
So final thing, if I may,

00:20:21.686 --> 00:20:22.860
I just want to talk a
bit about NHS England,

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:25.290
our organisation for a moment

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.570
because you will know colleagues,

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:31.800
that we've had quite a number
of colleagues now leave us

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:34.350
over the course of the
past couple of months,

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:37.290
or who are planning to leave
us over the next few weeks

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.350
as part of the new NHS England programme

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:42.210
that includes the last of those

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:45.390
who took voluntary
redundancy earlier this year

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:47.520
and colleagues who are
transferring to ICBs

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:51.480
to support them in their delegated duties.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.789
So I think on all of our behalfs,

00:20:53.789 --> 00:20:56.460
we just want to wish all
of those colleagues well

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.730
and thank them for their service.

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:02.700
95% of remaining colleagues
have now completed

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:05.250
or are currently in consultation.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:07.080
So the shape of our future organisation

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:10.110
and the impact on people
is becoming clearer,

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:12.750
but we do know that of course that means

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:16.200
it is now almost the maximum
period of uncertainty

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:19.620
for colleagues and that is inevitably

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.470
creating quite a lot of individual anxiety

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:25.860
for colleagues who we
just urge anyone who feels

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:27.150
that they would benefit from support

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:28.860
to access that through their line manager

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:31.172
or through the resources on the hub.

00:21:31.172 --> 00:21:32.640
And again, I'd just like to thank

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:37.260
all of my colleagues who
continue to work tirelessly

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.355
in service of the NHS,

00:21:39.355 --> 00:21:40.350
despite the fact that
they're going through

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:43.440
significant change
programme at the same time.

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:45.510
You very kindly said if
we're talking internally

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:46.800
that I could just say something

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:49.710
about Tim and David before I finish.

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:52.200
So I'm going to take that
opportunity if I may,

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:53.430
'cause talking about
change at executive level,

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:55.303
actually losing two
colleagues is a big thing.

00:21:55.303 --> 00:21:59.610
So an opportunity for me just
to say an enormous thank you

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:01.470
to both Tim and David.

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:03.367
Tim, I think when you joined us,

00:22:03.367 --> 00:22:06.810
you had not anticipated
that you would be leading

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:08.820
the scale of change programme

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.670
that you have in fact led us through.

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.200
But where are we now?

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.560
Well, not only I think is
there a real track record

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.900
that you can point to of
things that have been delivered

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:21.330
more EPRs just as a one example

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:24.480
than we've ever had going live
at any one time in the NHS.

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:26.580
But there's a really clear blueprint.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:28.620
We know exactly what we need to do

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:30.270
to put the basics in place,

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:32.250
but we also know where
we want to go beyond that

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:34.560
and we are beginning to actually do it.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:38.910
So I think a quite
remarkable period of change

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:40.861
over the last couple of years,

00:22:40.861 --> 00:22:42.390
but leaves us in a place where not only

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:44.910
can you point to a set of things

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.490
which have happened and are happening,

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:50.054
but a really clear
blueprint for the future.

00:22:50.054 --> 00:22:53.051
And we just want to say thank
you for that because that is,

00:22:53.051 --> 00:22:56.040
I think we would all
acknowledge if there is,

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:58.489
amongst the things we talked about,

00:22:58.489 --> 00:22:59.723
about how important workforce is,

00:23:00.739 --> 00:23:02.638
we talk about how important capital is

00:23:02.638 --> 00:23:03.935
in terms of buildings.

00:23:03.935 --> 00:23:05.856
But the other thing we
talk a lot about isn't it,

00:23:05.856 --> 00:23:08.917
is how critical having
the right digital data,

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:13.500
technical architecture and
the right vision for that

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:16.140
is going to be if we want
to deliver the ambitions

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:18.690
that we all have for
the NHS in the future.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:22.274
David, I think, sorry, you don't get away.

00:23:22.274 --> 00:23:26.193
Just want to say actually,

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.600
David is somebody who has
an extraordinary career

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.720
in the NHS and the esteem
in which you are held David

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:37.320
across the NHS reflects
not the last two years

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:40.413
but the last 40.

00:23:42.651 --> 00:23:43.963
- Yeah, the last few years.

00:23:43.963 --> 00:23:48.480
- Few years, and the way
that you lead with values,

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:51.180
integrity and total commitment

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.500
I think is a real role
model to other colleagues.

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:56.040
And I know that they have appreciated,

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.170
as I have personally
appreciated for the support

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.600
you have given me and
you have given colleagues

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:03.480
over the time that you've
been with us, as ever,

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:07.170
when the time comes to step
up you are there to do it

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:09.621
and you have done for the last 40 years.

00:24:09.621 --> 00:24:13.050
So an extraordinary track
record and we will really,

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:15.000
really miss both of you.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:18.570
So I also want to say,
if I may then in advance,

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:22.902
thank you to Vinda
Walker and to Jim Mackey

00:24:22.902 --> 00:24:25.470
who are stepping in to
lead through transition

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:28.080
to give them their full
titles, Dr. Vinda Walker

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.330
and Sir James Mackey.

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:33.900
But they will be with us next
time that we meet as a board

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:37.140
and we are actively in the
transition phase at the moment.

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:40.890
So recognising that you
don't replace two colleagues

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:43.950
like Tim and David easily, nonetheless,

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:48.180
hugely grateful to Vin
and to Jim for stepping up

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.313
in the way that they are already doing.

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:52.740
- Thank you.

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:56.550
So just before I then do
go to the next item, David,

00:24:56.550 --> 00:25:01.550
which will be you on
operational performance update.

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:05.310
Just actually to add
the non-executive thanks

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:09.060
to the execs actually this,

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:11.520
the merger and the headcount reductions

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:14.490
that have followed that have
been a significant strain

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:17.430
on the organisation and
in spite of that strain

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:19.939
actually you continue to lead a number,

00:25:19.939 --> 00:25:24.630
all the programmes actually
with great clarity and vigour.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:27.000
And so our thanks to you for that

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.340
and just continuing under
the very real pressure

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:31.090
actually of handling what is always

00:25:32.632 --> 00:25:34.710
a very difficult exercise.

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:37.007
And if I did forget to say,

00:25:37.007 --> 00:25:39.360
but I would just like
to say particular thanks

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:42.570
because the ambulance shift
that I did two nights or so ago,

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:45.330
I just do want to thank
the two crew members,

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:46.877
Adam Aston and Laura Cooper.

00:25:46.877 --> 00:25:49.890
Because whenever I am privileged enough

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:53.280
to go into the front line and
try and keep out of the way

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:54.420
as much as possible,

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:57.629
actually, you see the
most amazing commitment

00:25:57.629 --> 00:26:01.050
and skill levels and both of them actually

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:03.240
in terms of all the interventions

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:06.330
that we were called out to.

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:08.550
Not only do they show
huge skill and expertise,

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:10.980
but the patience and empathy
with which they engage

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:12.390
with the members of the public

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:14.803
to whom they're called,
it is remarkable to see,

00:26:14.803 --> 00:26:17.280
actually remarkable in its literal sense,

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:20.490
worthy of marking, absolutely
remarkable to see that.

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:23.130
And also tribute to the
emergency department staff

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:27.330
