00:26:49.118 --> 00:26:50.416
- Yes, thank you.

00:26:50.416 --> 00:26:51.723
So I will now present my final last ever

00:26:51.723 --> 00:26:52.920
operational report to the board.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.290
And before I do so can I say
thank you for those kind words,

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:58.200
thank you for 40 years, it's
been absolutely fantastic.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.750
Really rewarding, I wouldn't
have missed a second of it

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:02.820
and my best to you all.

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:04.780
Right, industrial action is the thing

00:27:06.241 --> 00:27:07.928
which has had the biggest impact

00:27:07.928 --> 00:27:08.761
on operational performance
over recent months.

00:27:08.761 --> 00:27:10.590
So I'll bring Navina in to start actually

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:12.300
to set the overall scene before I go back

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:14.280
into the detail of my report

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.380
and the impact on performance, so Navina.

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.020
- Thank you David, so I just
want to say three things.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:23.963
The first is that to mark
here in the board, the impact

00:27:23.963 --> 00:27:26.280
that the continued industrial action

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:30.210
is having on all our staff,
clinical, nonclinical,

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:33.090
people who have to manage
all the kind of logistics

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:38.090
and programmes and the planning
but also on all of our staff

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.050
who are taking industrial action

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:45.060
and the staff who are not.

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:48.570
So it's been a real toll on
people and we are beginning

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:50.520
to really feel that.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:52.350
So I just want to say that here

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:54.630
and also thank all of
our staff for continuing

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:56.040
to keep things going

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:58.597
as Richard has just described so vividly.

00:27:58.597 --> 00:27:59.910
The second point to make is,

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:04.784
and we are planning for this to continue

00:28:04.784 --> 00:28:06.630
for the foreseeable future.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:09.300
So that's the way in which we are thinking

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:12.681
and certainly it'll
continue through August.

00:28:12.681 --> 00:28:15.210
And the point about
August that I want to make

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:19.710
is that it we have got
junior doctors going on

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:22.170
or trainee doctors going on strike

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:24.270
and also consultants later in the month.

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:27.695
The point about August
is it's rotation time.

00:28:27.695 --> 00:28:31.366
So we are working really
closely with our trainees,

00:28:31.366 --> 00:28:34.632
with their trainers and
with the organisations

00:28:34.632 --> 00:28:36.000
to manage the fact

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:40.345
that it is at this point in
time when we have new doctors

00:28:40.345 --> 00:28:42.772
coming into the organisations,

00:28:42.772 --> 00:28:46.830
they're supported to
join the organisations.

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:49.770
This includes F1 doctors
and also the consultants

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:53.460
who will be supporting them
and managing the impact.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.010
So that's something that's particular

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.410
that we are dealing with
at the moment for August.

00:28:58.410 --> 00:28:59.243
Thank you David.

00:29:00.249 --> 00:29:01.565
- Okay, thank you Navina.

00:29:01.565 --> 00:29:02.430
Okay, so returning to my report,

00:29:02.430 --> 00:29:04.470
as you'll see the biggest
impact industrial action

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:06.185
has been having today is on planned care.

00:29:06.185 --> 00:29:10.830
And we have rescheduled over
half a million appointments

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.360
for inpatients or outpatients

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:13.560
and is worth bearing in mind

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:16.260
that we would also reduced
planned activities,

00:29:16.260 --> 00:29:18.840
a consequence of seeing the
industrial actual was coming.

00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:21.487
So the impact will be
greater than that number.

00:29:21.487 --> 00:29:25.320
We have remained nonetheless
very, very focused

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:27.090
on the long waits and we've continued

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:30.150
to make progress on both 104 week waiters,

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:33.284
the 78 week waiters and
the 65 week waiters.

00:29:33.284 --> 00:29:37.200
So 104 week waiters are
now down to 482 patients,

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.350
which is a 94% reduction
on the previous year,

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:43.200
78 week waiters in May it was

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:45.529
nearly 11 and a half thousand,

00:29:45.529 --> 00:29:47.520
which was an 80% reduction
on the previous years.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:49.547
And on 65 week waiters,

00:29:49.547 --> 00:29:52.230
there's a 30% improvement
on previous years

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:54.920
and we are heading in the right direction

00:29:54.920 --> 00:29:55.800
as we work our way through the year.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.750
Nonetheless, it is having an impact

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.820
on the overall waiting list in the round

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:01.920
and that's where the biggest
impact is being noticed.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:05.790
And also on the 52 week
waiters and in May,

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:07.268
total waiting list was up to seven,

00:30:07.268 --> 00:30:09.750
almost seven and a half million,

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:13.260
7,472,000 patients were
on the list in May.

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:16.020
So that's where the biggest
impact is being felt.

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:18.000
But we are remaining very, very focused

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:19.582
on long waits.

00:30:19.582 --> 00:30:23.236
Elsewhere in the urgent care pathway,

00:30:23.236 --> 00:30:27.493
we have remained fairly
resilient actually.

00:30:27.493 --> 00:30:28.440
So in terms of the two headline metrics

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:30.204
in terms of the 76% for our target,

00:30:30.204 --> 00:30:35.204
that's running at around 74% during June

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:38.764
and that's continued into July,

00:30:38.764 --> 00:30:40.980
which is actually ahead of trajectory,

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:43.530
it's ahead of where we
thought we would be on 76%.

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:46.380
Nonetheless, we must
remind ourselves that 76%

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:48.420
is a staging post onto better performance,

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:51.660
but we are absolutely focused
on delivery in that space.

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:55.920
And on the category
two 30 minute handover,

00:30:55.920 --> 00:31:00.240
the six weeks rolling average
is now 34 minutes, 42 seconds,

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:03.030
which again, this time last
year it was near a 50 minutes.

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:05.070
So again, it's real, real progress

00:31:05.070 --> 00:31:06.930
and as we will come on to shortly

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.660
in terms of the winter plan
which has been launched today,

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:11.940
those are the interventions
in place to ensure

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:14.010
that we double down on what we need

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:16.463
to do to deliver that 30 minutes

00:31:16.463 --> 00:31:19.773
and 76% as we move into the winter.

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:23.015
I'm not going to run through
the whole of the rest

00:31:23.015 --> 00:31:25.020
of my report Richard,
happy to take questions.

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:27.780
Just just to note on
cancer actually two things.

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:30.870
It's worth looking at
we're still at almost 130%

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:33.257
of referrals of pre pandemic,

00:31:33.257 --> 00:31:36.000
which is actually exactly
where we would want it to be

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:38.250
and it's putting pressure
on the diagnostic capacity,

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:41.478
but we are determined to
ensure that those pathways flow

00:31:41.478 --> 00:31:44.370
and at the end of quarter one,

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:46.500
we were slightly ahead of target

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:48.810
on the 62 day backlog performance

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:51.870
and on the faster diagnosis performance,

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:56.370
we are currently at 71.3%
against the 75% standard,

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:59.100
which is ahead of the 69% staging post

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.950
we thought we would be
at, at this point in time.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:06.373
So it is a system which remains busy.

00:32:06.373 --> 00:32:08.823
You will notice the
attendances in A and E in June

00:32:08.823 --> 00:32:10.620
were the highest they've
ever been in any June.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:12.849
The referrals continue to be up,

00:32:12.849 --> 00:32:15.104
which is what we would want them to be.

00:32:15.104 --> 00:32:16.650
We are being hit by industrial action.

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:19.380
But thanks to the hard work
of colleagues out there,

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:22.121
we are remaining in a reasonable place

00:32:22.121 --> 00:32:24.660
on overall performance in the round.

00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:26.850
So I'll pauses there, but
happy to take questions

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:29.760
that any colleagues would wish to raise.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:33.933
- So Andrew, I think Andrew
and then David, yeah.

00:32:34.844 --> 00:32:37.860
- Thanks Richard, thanks David.

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:42.390
So really good to see the
performance on urgent care

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:45.330
moving in the right
direction, really positive.

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:49.980
Just be grateful for a
comment on 12 hour waits,

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:53.610
whether that's going up, down or flat.

00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:55.710
And secondly, just to give us a reflection

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:57.000
on the cancer workload

00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:00.042
'cause that's obviously
putting a lot of pressure

00:33:00.042 --> 00:33:02.130
on providers and the capacity they earmark

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:07.130
for cancer referrals and
just projecting that forward,

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:09.630
what do you think it's going to be like

00:33:10.613 --> 00:33:12.652
hitting the 62 day
standard in 62 days time,

00:33:12.652 --> 00:33:14.130
if you see what I mean?

00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:15.690
- Yeah, yeah,

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.430
I'll do those in reverse
orders then Andrew.

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:19.890
So the cancer, there is some seasonality

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:21.284
to cancer referrals actually.

00:33:21.284 --> 00:33:24.724
So the numbers are slightly
ticking up at the moment,

00:33:24.724 --> 00:33:28.590
but we believe we do have
the capacity in place

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:30.131
to ensure at year end,

00:33:30.131 --> 00:33:32.610
we will be on track and on target

00:33:32.610 --> 00:33:35.790
to deliver the quantum of 62
day backlog that we plan for

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:38.853
and that we will be hitting
the faster diagnostic standard.

00:33:38.853 --> 00:33:42.330
You'll be aware that the
diagnostic investment programme

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:44.760
that we've had this year
is materially increasing

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:46.260
the amount of diagnostic capacity

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.900
that we have in place,
which is fundamental,

00:33:48.900 --> 00:33:51.180
you need your diagnostics
upstream as you work your way

00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:54.780
through the pathway and we're
ensuring that the modalities

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:56.430
are aligned to cancer demand.

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:58.890
So there's an absolute linkage
between new capacity opening

00:33:58.890 --> 00:33:59.723
and demand.

00:33:59.723 --> 00:34:03.630
So I think we are in a
reasonable place this year,

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:05.820
but that demand as it
continues into next year,

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:08.222
we need to ensure that
we continue to align

00:34:08.222 --> 00:34:09.960
that demand and capacity.

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:12.689
But I think on cancer
in a reasonable place

00:34:12.689 --> 00:34:16.551
with a forward look. On the 12 hour waits,

00:34:16.551 --> 00:34:18.555
so my colleague Sarah
Jane might want to come in

00:34:18.555 --> 00:34:19.388
on this actually she's here today.

00:34:19.388 --> 00:34:21.458
There has been an increase
in 12 hour waits actually.

00:34:21.458 --> 00:34:23.730
And when we get into the next discussion

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:25.860
about the winter plan, we need to ensure

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:26.970
that those interventions

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:29.251
aren't just hitting those
two headline metrics,

00:34:29.251 --> 00:34:31.680
but beneath that we are driving

00:34:31.680 --> 00:34:34.674
the 12 hour waits within the departments.

00:34:34.674 --> 00:34:37.228
- Yeah, I mean just briefly Andrew,

00:34:37.228 --> 00:34:38.250
the range here is quite significant.

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:40.470
So in our sort of better
performing organisations,

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:42.743
we can see about a three, 4% figure.

00:34:42.743 --> 00:34:45.480
But in some of the organisations
that really struggle,

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:49.560
it can go as high as 20% during
periods and they're the ones

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.806
that we really worried about,
as part of the winter letter

00:34:51.806 --> 00:34:54.330
that's now been published,

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:55.660
I'm pleased to say,

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:58.727
one of the things that
we are doing through that

00:34:58.727 --> 00:35:00.150
is incentivizing organisations

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:02.310
around ambulance handover delay

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:05.094
and improving an ambulance handover delay

00:35:05.094 --> 00:35:07.437
and the only real way to do that

00:35:07.437 --> 00:35:09.445
in a sustainable way, as you will know,

00:35:09.445 --> 00:35:12.264
is to reduce the department for 12 hours

00:35:12.264 --> 00:35:13.613
to give you the capacity to pull through.

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:16.178
This is not about just
getting people off the back

00:35:16.178 --> 00:35:17.793
of ambulances and
putting them in the front

00:35:17.793 --> 00:35:19.104
of A&amp;E departments.

00:35:19.104 --> 00:35:20.520
It's about that flow
and that pull through.

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:23.190
So a big focus on 12 hours going forwards.

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:26.280
I've actually been in the
southwest for the last three days

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:28.680
and they are making
some steady improvements

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:30.450
on handover delays and they're doing it

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:31.889
by focusing on 12 hours in department.

00:35:31.889 --> 00:35:33.892
So it is an issue,

00:35:33.892 --> 00:35:37.410
there is a big variation but
it's one of our key priorities

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:38.640
going into winter.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:39.900
- Thank you.

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:42.497
- David, you were going to ask a question

00:35:42.497 --> 00:35:43.330
I think or make a point?

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:44.693
- I may, there's two different areas

00:35:44.693 --> 00:35:46.881
I wouldn't mind raising,

00:35:46.881 --> 00:35:49.830
in the cover report, the main report,

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:51.810
this might be for you Sarah Jane,

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:54.079
paragraph 18 talks about
the reasons for delay

00:35:54.079 --> 00:35:59.079
of people in hospital and references

00:35:59.889 --> 00:36:02.643
the relationship to social care.

00:36:03.752 --> 00:36:05.610
And I'm just curious, with vacancies

