00:37:47.231 --> 00:37:49.950
And I just wonder whether
we can bring some work

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:53.821
back on what we can do in relation to,

00:37:53.821 --> 00:37:54.870
I'm not saying this because,

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:56.520
Habib is quoted in the press release.

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:58.590
I didn't know Habib was going to hear

00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:01.812
when I scribbled over my report.

00:38:01.812 --> 00:38:03.233
It's a good thing he is here.

00:38:03.233 --> 00:38:07.113
That's the most shocking
figure I've heard this week.

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:09.593
- Yeah.

00:38:10.943 --> 00:38:12.930
- And actually it just left
me to say come on Behan,

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:15.180
you're a board member,
what are you going to do?

00:38:16.371 --> 00:38:17.676
There's got to be a question at this board

00:38:17.676 --> 00:38:20.370
about what we're prepared
to do about that.

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:21.870
So again, I'm sorry,

00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:23.850
it sounds like a statement,
I don't mean it to do that,

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:25.980
but it's in the report,
there's tremendous progress

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:28.980
and my ask is can we bring something back

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:31.680
at a future meeting about what we're doing

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:36.300
to provide some further development work

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:40.854
to address that particular
figure, which I find shocking.

00:38:40.854 --> 00:38:42.151
- I mean do you want to react?

00:38:42.151 --> 00:38:43.461
But I think they're very good,

00:38:43.461 --> 00:38:44.430
both of those actually very
good ideas to come back

00:38:44.430 --> 00:38:46.380
with some better understanding.

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:47.430
- Just briefly sorry
Richard, yes, of course.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:49.200
I will respond briefly in reverse.

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:50.498
You are right David, we
have made real progress

00:38:50.498 --> 00:38:52.740
on the annual health checks actually

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:54.660
and there's been a real focus of work

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:56.700
and I pay tribute to
GP colleagues out there

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:58.560
in terms of who've absolutely
doubled down on this.

00:38:58.560 --> 00:38:59.630
The last point to make,

00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:01.770
it is a truly shocking figure actually

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:02.730
and perhaps Bola

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:04.531
may wish to comment when we
come onto some work later,

00:39:04.531 --> 00:39:06.494
but I will certainly come
back and work with Bola

00:39:06.494 --> 00:39:10.640
and indeed Tom actually to
bring a piece of work back.

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:12.630
So we shed more light on that,

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:15.080
in terms of complex comorbidities
and length of stage,

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:17.040
do you have insight into that?

00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:20.044
- I absolutely agree with
what you've said, David.

00:39:20.044 --> 00:39:21.240
The patients that are waiting the longest

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:22.830
after they're medically fit for discharge

00:39:22.830 --> 00:39:24.450
to leave hospital are people

00:39:24.450 --> 00:39:27.000
with complex comorbidities and dementia

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:30.300
that cannot just go to any nursing home.

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:32.850
'Cause often there's
nursing home care available

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:36.540
in most areas, but for specialist care,

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:38.310
it can be very difficult to access.

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:39.720
And when we look at our longest,

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:42.300
the people are in sort of over 50 days.

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:45.600
It's precisely that
group that you describe.

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:47.550
- I'm told there's research

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:51.000
that says the biggest predictor of delays

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:55.020
is dementia and that's
why people are stuck.

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:57.060
So it's a complexity story.

00:39:57.060 --> 00:39:59.790
So the capacity needs
to match the complexity.

00:39:59.790 --> 00:40:01.740
- Let's not bring those people to hospital

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:03.720
from where they are if there's any way

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:05.851
of avoiding that and that's a big,

00:40:05.851 --> 00:40:08.602
I'll come onto that, but
that's a big drive here.

00:40:08.602 --> 00:40:09.917
Let's keep people where they are

00:40:09.917 --> 00:40:11.223
and support them as best we can.

00:40:11.223 --> 00:40:12.390
We are only going to send
them down the wrong path

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:15.487
if we bring them to
hospital in many instances.

00:40:15.487 --> 00:40:17.010
- But on both of those
questions, can we take them away,

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:18.120
have a look at, and we will come back

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:19.847
to the board on those,
either in the private board

00:40:19.847 --> 00:40:22.276
or the public board actually,

00:40:22.276 --> 00:40:23.951
but we should definitely do that while

00:40:23.951 --> 00:40:25.249
you were going to to ask.

00:40:25.249 --> 00:40:26.567
- Yeah, thank you Richard.

00:40:26.567 --> 00:40:27.600
I really welcome,

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:29.820
there's the publication
of winter plan so early,

00:40:29.820 --> 00:40:31.170
I mean it's a first,

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.067
it's something which
colleagues have been around

00:40:34.067 --> 00:40:37.313
the board for a little
while have been calling for

00:40:37.313 --> 00:40:38.310
and we're delighted it's come.

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:40.550
My question really is about
the industrial action.

00:40:40.550 --> 00:40:42.540
If that continues into winter,

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:44.490
and maybe you'll take
it the next conversation

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:46.939
when we're talking about winter plan,

00:40:46.939 --> 00:40:48.030
but if we pull together the electives

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:49.080
and the progress we're making,

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:51.450
particularly the long, long wait,

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:52.950
but still the numbers are going up.

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:54.960
If you then think about industrial action,

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:58.140
which may continue, have
we sufficiently modelled

00:40:58.140 --> 00:41:00.420
and thought about how we have to respond?

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:02.225
'Cause that's our role here,

00:41:02.225 --> 00:41:06.750
to respond to this action
because in my head anyway,

00:41:06.750 --> 00:41:08.220
something's got to give if you like,

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:10.800
if you are focusing on
urgent emergency care

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:13.568
in winter, then does elective suffer?

00:41:13.568 --> 00:41:15.949
If so, how much, just so we understand

00:41:15.949 --> 00:41:18.720
and everyone's working very, very hard.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:20.820
There are lots of complex
issues around this,

00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:22.293
but in our winter planning,

00:41:22.293 --> 00:41:26.520
have we really drilled
down and thought about it?

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:28.020
- Okay, I'll reflect back on that.

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:29.283
We might want to discuss
that a little bit more

00:41:29.283 --> 00:41:33.120
when we get into the
plan, but let's be clear,

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:34.410
the industrial action at the moment,

00:41:34.410 --> 00:41:37.110
the biggest impact is on
the planned care workload.

00:41:37.110 --> 00:41:41.340
And if winter pressures
as they ramp up again,

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:44.040
we will prioritise patient
safety in the urgent care pathway

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:45.780
because that will be the absolute priority

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:48.690
and consequentially, it has the potential

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:50.760
to have a bigger impact on planned care.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.620
So that is where we would go.

00:41:52.620 --> 00:41:54.660
Now every time we do this,

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:56.580
we are getting better at it actually.

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.440
And it's not a learned skill

00:41:58.440 --> 00:42:00.360
that I particularly enjoy
having to be honest with you.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.322
That's the way it works.

00:42:02.322 --> 00:42:05.740
So we are managing to keep
more of the elective show

00:42:05.740 --> 00:42:07.590
on the road.

00:42:07.590 --> 00:42:09.810
As Navina has said, I don't
think we should underestimate

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:11.970
the impact of five days
of junior doctor action

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:14.190
than two consultants during
the last two weeks of August.

00:42:14.190 --> 00:42:15.996
That will be profound.

00:42:15.996 --> 00:42:18.240
But the impact will continue

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:20.100
to be on the planned care

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.770
and the proportionality of
that impact will be contingent

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:25.320
on many other winter factors obviously.

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:26.153
- Thanks.

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:30.723
- Any other comments or questions
for David or Sarah Jane?

00:42:31.800 --> 00:42:33.751
Thank you very much, thank you.

00:42:33.751 --> 00:42:37.590
So Julian, I think financial
performance update.

00:42:37.590 --> 00:42:39.790
- So on the financial performance,

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:42.510
I think last time on the
board we discussed the plans

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:45.750
we had agreed with the 42 systems.

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:48.810
Those do include our plans for 15 systems

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:50.590
where we have effectively agreed

00:42:52.117 --> 00:42:57.117
spending over and above the
absolute allocation we gave them

00:42:57.386 --> 00:43:02.386
at the end of month two we
were running about £300 million

00:43:04.389 --> 00:43:06.963
in deficit against the
kind of planned trajectory,

00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:10.636
some of that is because of
the way people have phased

00:43:10.636 --> 00:43:12.432
the implementation

00:43:12.432 --> 00:43:15.420
of some of their plans in their budgets.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:19.710
So I think probably
overstate where they are

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:21.720
against what they're
really planning to do.

00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:24.630
But I do think, people are off.

00:43:24.630 --> 00:43:28.890
There is a large element of
that which is due to the cost

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:33.890
of the strikes, in particular
as the paper notes,

00:43:35.704 --> 00:43:39.300
the total monetary cost of the strikes

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:41.733
looks like it is around for the strikes,

00:43:42.641 --> 00:43:45.780
the junior doctor strike in
April, around £315 million.

00:43:45.780 --> 00:43:48.480
That breaks down to
roughly a third of that

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:52.500
is due to just lost activity on the days

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:56.160
which you would have to recover
by paying for higher cost,

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:59.880
higher premium and
initiatives later the year.

00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:03.240
A third of that, roughly
100 million pounds,

00:44:03.240 --> 00:44:06.120
is to the direct net cost

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:09.378
of effectively paying more senior staff

00:44:09.378 --> 00:44:12.300
to cover junior staff
who have gone on strike

00:44:12.300 --> 00:44:15.000
who you are paying at premium rates

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:17.490
and probably about a third of the cost

00:44:17.490 --> 00:44:20.490
is down to disruption, by disruption

00:44:20.490 --> 00:44:24.210
I include actually the
fact that those staff

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:26.970
who are covering others
have often deferred leave,

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:29.160
which they will take later in the year

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:32.070
or indeed a lot of management
and clinical attention

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:34.680
that would go on delivering
some of the savings

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:37.320
that we need or other change

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:40.020
that we are looking to
deliver has been consumed

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:43.920
in the management and preparation
for that strike activity.

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:48.920
Now we have agreed with
government that as a result,

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:52.350
we cannot afford to do
as much elective activity

00:44:52.350 --> 00:44:55.650
across the whole year as
we had originally planned.

00:44:55.650 --> 00:44:57.060
And we have agreed that we have reduced

00:44:57.060 --> 00:45:00.360
the elective activity goal
by 2% across the whole year.

00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:03.061
So we'd asked people to do about 107%

00:45:03.061 --> 00:45:04.830
of their 1920 activity and we've agreed,

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:06.720
this is the impact of the April strike

00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:08.707
that becomes 105%.

00:45:08.707 --> 00:45:13.707
And we had communicated that
about a week and a half ago

00:45:14.172 --> 00:45:19.172
to ICBs and to providers and
we are currently in discussion

00:45:21.030 --> 00:45:26.030
with government about how we
deal with the June strikes

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:32.060
and the July strikes and then
if there's further action,

00:45:32.222 --> 00:45:34.080
how we will deal with those

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:38.370
because we have been clear
throughout the planning process

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:40.200
that if circumstances change,

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:42.360
we will need to adjust the plans

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:45.386
that we have asked people to deliver.

00:45:45.386 --> 00:45:50.386
Clearly we are also working
with systems who are further off

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:55.132
against the financial
trajectory compared to others

00:45:55.132 --> 00:45:58.230
and making sure that
proper control processes

00:45:58.230 --> 00:45:59.844
are in place in some places

00:45:59.844 --> 00:46:02.090
that they have the right support,

00:46:02.090 --> 00:46:04.590
either provided directly by us

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:09.590
or sourced by them from outside providers.

00:46:09.711 --> 00:46:14.711
And we are continuing to
develop the tools to help people

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:17.730
identify where they can
deliver better value for money.

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:22.730
We are in the process
of rolling out new data

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:25.634
on medicine spend which
enables every trust,

00:46:25.634 --> 00:46:29.700
every secondary acute trust in the country

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:32.550
to identify where there are opportunities

00:46:32.550 --> 00:46:35.280
for better prescribing patterns,

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:37.471
switching to best value medicines,

00:46:37.471 --> 00:46:40.410
to cheaper biosimilars.

00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:42.660
So we are in the process
of rolling out their tools

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:45.150
to help people deliver against the plans

00:46:45.150 --> 00:46:47.583
that they have committed to.

00:46:49.650 --> 00:46:51.810
The other major issue to note

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:55.350
is that clearly since the last board,

00:46:55.350 --> 00:46:57.570
the government, both
published and accepted

00:46:57.570 --> 00:47:01.173
the recommendations of the
doctor's pay review body,

00:47:02.071 --> 00:47:07.071
we were funded for 3% pay settlement

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:09.470
across all our staff groups.

00:47:10.377 --> 00:47:12.150
As we have talked about
at previous boards,

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:14.250
the DDRB recommendations

00:47:14.250 --> 00:47:16.893
are clearly significantly
more expensive than that.

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:20.550
The department has been clear

00:47:20.550 --> 00:47:22.830
that they will fund us for those.

00:47:22.830 --> 00:47:27.830
So we will fully fund
all systems and providers

00:47:29.490 --> 00:47:31.260
for the cost of those pay deals.

00:47:31.260 --> 00:47:32.400
We've been clear throughout

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:35.520
that you can't just simply
keep adding on pressure

00:47:35.520 --> 00:47:37.470
to what we've asked systems to do.

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:40.050
And we will follow through on the basis

00:47:40.050 --> 00:47:41.722
we ask people to plan,

00:47:41.722 --> 00:47:43.590
which we will fully fund those pay deals.

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:45.342
By and large,

00:47:45.342 --> 00:47:49.375
the department is covering
the cost of those through,

00:47:49.375 --> 00:47:52.410
as I think has been said in the media,

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:55.440
the immigration and health surcharge

00:47:55.440 --> 00:47:59.409
and through some reprioritization
within the department.

00:47:59.409 --> 00:48:02.950
There have been in discussion with us

00:48:03.816 --> 00:48:06.100
about whether we can make a contribution

00:48:06.100 --> 00:48:09.243
towards the pay costs to the
tune of about £100 million,

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:13.740
at this point, they've asked
us to not take the active steps

00:48:13.740 --> 00:48:17.810
to contribute to that in 23/24,

00:48:17.810 --> 00:48:21.776
they are looking at how
they manage that pressure

00:48:21.776 --> 00:48:26.310
as a risk across the whole
departmental position.

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:31.232
So clearly we will need to
come back to their ask of us

00:48:31.232 --> 00:48:35.250
in 24, 25 and they'll
need to be discussing

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:36.610
with the treasury

00:48:38.083 --> 00:48:41.052
how that pressure is
also covered in 24, 25.

00:48:41.052 --> 00:48:46.052
So if we are asked to find, for example,

00:48:47.017 --> 00:48:49.495
£100 million in 24 25
against our existing plans,

00:48:49.495 --> 00:48:53.250
we've just been clear we're at the stage

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:57.450
where we will have to cut
national investment programmes

00:48:57.450 --> 00:48:59.210
that we have otherwise planned.

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:02.640
And I think we have discussed
this at the board previously

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:05.370
and nothing on that
score has really changed.

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:07.113
So I think that is one
we will come back to

00:49:07.113 --> 00:49:10.593
as we look at planning for 24, 25.

00:49:11.850 --> 00:49:14.490
Beyond that, actually
the capital programmes

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:16.080
are going reasonably well,

00:49:16.080 --> 00:49:19.950
the investment into community diagnostics,

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:23.793
extra elective capacity,

00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:28.653
the technology programmes
that are being led by Tim,

00:49:30.210 --> 00:49:32.460
clearly there is enormous
pressure for capital spend

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:34.710
within systems and we'll just keep working

00:49:34.710 --> 00:49:38.514
with the NHS and looking forward

00:49:38.514 --> 00:49:42.510
as to how we can support people to deal

00:49:42.510 --> 00:49:44.183
with the pressures they're facing.

00:49:45.180 --> 00:49:46.440
- Very good.

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:48.336
Actually we did, didn't we,

00:49:48.336 --> 00:49:49.440
we had a very good
conversation where Steve,

00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:52.290
Steve Russell, this mornings,

00:49:52.290 --> 00:49:56.830
well actually was early afternoons
part of the private board

00:49:57.892 --> 00:50:01.260
on diagnostics and screening
and just to that point actually

00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:04.350
about investment and so on,
we're going to have what,

00:50:04.350 --> 00:50:07.842
173 or so community diagnostic centres

00:50:07.842 --> 00:50:09.480
by the end of the year.

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:13.786
So we got 111 at the moment and
62 more opening by December.

00:50:13.786 --> 00:50:16.260
That's a significant step
forward in that capacity.

00:50:16.260 --> 00:50:19.440
So that'd be really good to
see those come on stream.

00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:21.390
Any questions or comments?

00:50:21.390 --> 00:50:24.240
Reactions for Julian on
the financials, Andrew?

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:25.890
- So Julian on inflation,

00:50:25.890 --> 00:50:29.673
to what extent does that dented
our position going forward?

00:50:31.170 --> 00:50:35.580
- So it is running higher than,

00:50:37.950 --> 00:50:42.420
when we were agreeing funding
with the treasury in November

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:45.810
last year and setting
the planning guidance

00:50:45.810 --> 00:50:48.003
over December and January,

00:50:50.610 --> 00:50:52.320
we are just trying to assess

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:55.770
how much higher and in which areas,

00:50:55.770 --> 00:50:59.400
because clearly the way
the government looks at it

00:50:59.400 --> 00:51:01.750
for the whole public
sector is the GDP deflator

00:51:03.108 --> 00:51:04.424
that is not regularly published.

00:51:04.424 --> 00:51:07.440
CPI is a reasonable measure

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:11.010
but is not quite measuring the same thing.

00:51:11.010 --> 00:51:15.030
But at the moment my
assessment is that potentially

00:51:15.030 --> 00:51:18.620
that is creating up to £900 million

00:51:18.620 --> 00:51:21.160
of pressure over and above

00:51:23.580 --> 00:51:27.660
what the forecast would've
said at the turn of the year.

00:51:27.660 --> 00:51:29.400
And again, we are working through

00:51:29.400 --> 00:51:33.059
with systems the impact of
that and how that is managed

00:51:33.059 --> 00:51:34.860
in the round.

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:37.530
- So I mean this must be
a really tough environment

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:39.840
for most boards to actually manage

00:51:39.840 --> 00:51:41.520
and I think we've got to recognise that

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:44.490
because you've got all of the
issues around the strikes,

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.490
inflation trying to
get rid of the backlog,

00:51:47.490 --> 00:51:50.615
trying to do the best for their workforce

00:51:50.615 --> 00:51:53.190
in terms of the people promise.

00:51:53.190 --> 00:51:55.410
So I think we've got to recognise the fact

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:57.630
that it's really tough out there and tough

00:51:57.630 --> 00:51:59.553
for all boards to manage this.

00:52:00.584 --> 00:52:03.130
- I completely agree with
your statement there Andrew,

00:52:03.130 --> 00:52:05.764
but I just want to ask a few the question

00:52:05.764 --> 00:52:06.720
on sort of where he thinks the balance is

00:52:06.720 --> 00:52:11.720
between the sort of pre
COVID business as usual

00:52:11.790 --> 00:52:14.850
getting stuff done, the
normal way people operate,

00:52:14.850 --> 00:52:17.730
which we need to get back
to and these new pressures

00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:19.590
coming on board because
we were doing really well

00:52:19.590 --> 00:52:21.660
getting back to that sort of baseline

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:23.850
of normal operating procedure.

00:52:23.850 --> 00:52:26.550
How much is led off of course, if at all?

00:52:26.550 --> 00:52:28.401
- Can I add to Wol's question,

00:52:28.401 --> 00:52:30.512
are there any productivity
improvements we can do

00:52:30.512 --> 00:52:33.162
to get back to where that
productivity was pre COVID?

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:38.917
- So just in terms of getting back

00:52:38.917 --> 00:52:40.234
to where we were pre COVID,

00:52:40.234 --> 00:52:42.390
I mean in one sense we won't
get back to where we were

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:45.546
pre COVID because we will not be operating

00:52:45.546 --> 00:52:47.891
under the same circumstances,
COVID is with us,

00:52:47.891 --> 00:52:51.360
it has changed the pattern
of demand for healthcare.

00:52:51.360 --> 00:52:56.360
This is more about how we are
adapting to living with COVID

00:52:57.480 --> 00:53:01.035
rather than a return to
what happened before.

00:53:01.035 --> 00:53:02.721
And I think that is really quite important

00:53:02.721 --> 00:53:04.187
for people to understand

00:53:04.187 --> 00:53:06.284
because it affects the patterns of care,

00:53:06.284 --> 00:53:07.581
the patterns of demand,

00:53:07.581 --> 00:53:08.414
the pathways we run,

00:53:08.414 --> 00:53:10.140
the impact of infection control regimes

00:53:10.140 --> 00:53:14.913
and indeed it affects
levels of sickness absence.

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:19.260
Nevertheless, given
that, if you were to look

00:53:19.260 --> 00:53:23.250
at the acute sector today, first of all,

00:53:23.250 --> 00:53:25.710
again the thing you got to
clock is one of the big things

00:53:25.710 --> 00:53:28.136
which could be perceived
as a drag on productivity

00:53:28.136 --> 00:53:31.170
is the increase in length of stay

00:53:31.170 --> 00:53:33.390
that we have seen and therefore,

00:53:33.390 --> 00:53:36.390
we are actually funding a much greater,

00:53:36.390 --> 00:53:37.800
higher number of bed days,

00:53:37.800 --> 00:53:42.480
which has a cost but our
throughput looks like it is lower.

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:46.325
Now again, that is in part
because of the difficulties

00:53:46.325 --> 00:53:48.270
that have been experiencing,

00:53:48.270 --> 00:53:50.430
getting people out of hospital,

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:54.390
often back into their home
or into care home beds.

00:53:54.390 --> 00:53:57.150
And this is not a critique
of local authority colleagues

00:53:57.150 --> 00:53:59.910
who are working incredibly well
and incredibly hard with us.

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:02.336
It's just a statement of the reality

00:54:02.336 --> 00:54:05.149
we are seeing progress on that

00:54:05.149 --> 00:54:08.636
and the support the
government has put in has been

00:54:08.636 --> 00:54:11.730
definitely really helpful in this regard.

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:13.920
We are steadily seeing the
number of people stuck in beds

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:17.610
for more than seven and more
than 14 days coming down.

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:21.470
Now if you were to look at
and actually wait properly,

00:54:21.470 --> 00:54:23.670
the number of people
we are basically seeing

00:54:23.670 --> 00:54:25.800
in emergency departments

00:54:25.800 --> 00:54:28.380
and then discharging them
without an admission,

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:31.650
whereas previously we'd have
admitted them, probably,

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:33.630
you know, we're just looking at this,

00:54:33.630 --> 00:54:35.430
our productivity would look a lot better.

00:54:35.430 --> 00:54:39.226
So the first place is we
just need to keep a focus

00:54:39.226 --> 00:54:40.350
on how we're getting
flow through hospital,

00:54:40.350 --> 00:54:42.540
how we're getting people out of hospital.

00:54:42.540 --> 00:54:44.556
We have seen transformation

00:54:44.556 --> 00:54:45.600
in pathways around same
day emergency care.

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:48.960
We need to make sure we are
counting these things properly.

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:53.960
So we're transforming the
way we care for people

00:54:54.472 --> 00:54:58.440
and we are getting after the problems

00:54:58.440 --> 00:55:00.570
that we have seen as we
have come out of COVID

00:55:00.570 --> 00:55:02.220
and we are recovering.

00:55:02.220 --> 00:55:05.425
And then clearly if you
then turn to the other area

00:55:05.425 --> 00:55:06.900
where people see it in planned care,

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:08.760
actually I would say we
have seen a step back up

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:12.913
in activity, if I look at
May and June, we are running,

00:55:12.913 --> 00:55:14.760
I mean in the first cut of the data,

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:16.610
two or three percentage points higher

00:55:17.515 --> 00:55:19.050
in terms of the total
activity that we were doing,

00:55:19.050 --> 00:55:20.490
in the context of the disruption

00:55:20.490 --> 00:55:23.609
we've seen actually I think
that is reasonably impressive.

00:55:23.609 --> 00:55:27.600
So it's just to keep people,

00:55:27.600 --> 00:55:30.540
despite all the challenges,
focused on that,

00:55:30.540 --> 00:55:34.350
it is why seeking a path
out of industrial action

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:39.075
to create stability where
we can, frankly, clinicians,

00:55:39.075 --> 00:55:43.050
medics, nurses, management can really have

00:55:43.050 --> 00:55:48.050
a stable period to focus on achieving care

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:52.320
and the changes we are
looking for, for example,

00:55:52.320 --> 00:55:56.820
how can we deliver more
patient initiated follow ups

00:55:56.820 --> 00:56:00.840
and free up staff to get focused on those

00:56:00.840 --> 00:56:02.520
who are sort of waiting

00:56:02.520 --> 00:56:05.010
to be seen and diagnosed
in the first place.

00:56:05.010 --> 00:56:07.770
So those are the things we are focused on,

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:12.106
but trying to get to a period of stability

00:56:12.106 --> 00:56:13.859
I think is going to be pretty key.

00:56:13.859 --> 00:56:17.679
- Can I just ask one very quick question?

00:56:17.679 --> 00:56:22.050
It's on technology budget and
we've had these conversations.

00:56:22.050 --> 00:56:27.050
I think, we arguably
spend less on technology,

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:29.080
half or so I think is the number

00:56:30.524 --> 00:56:33.570
that Western health systems
spend on technology.

00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:35.640
And so the question really is,

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:37.560
I think I know what the
answer's going to be,

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:40.920
which is yes to best of
your and the team's ability,

00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:44.020
but we are doing our best to
protect the technology budget

00:56:45.122 --> 00:56:47.730
through this time in
spite of what Andrew's

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:51.210
quite correct depiction of
the pressures upon the system.

00:56:51.210 --> 00:56:55.560
- So we absolutely are
seeking to protect it

00:56:55.560 --> 00:57:00.560
and we nationally spend
around £2 billion a year

00:57:01.440 --> 00:57:04.440
on technology, we are spending more,

00:57:04.440 --> 00:57:06.030
we are on track to spend more this year

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:09.120
than we have historically, I mean we think

00:57:09.120 --> 00:57:13.520
we need to materially
further increase that.

00:57:13.520 --> 00:57:15.360
And that actually is a
big part of what we said

00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:17.174
in the long term workforce plan.

00:57:17.174 --> 00:57:20.070
But amidst all the pressures
we are trying to hold onto

00:57:20.070 --> 00:57:22.365
making sure we are laying

00:57:22.365 --> 00:57:24.671
the right foundations now,

00:57:24.671 --> 00:57:28.620
the problem is the margin
of the budget gets squeezed.

00:57:28.620 --> 00:57:29.933
- [Richard] Yeah, where do you go?

00:57:31.679 --> 00:57:32.994
- The place we have to go

00:57:32.994 --> 00:57:35.149
because it's the place where
we are spending material money

00:57:35.149 --> 00:57:37.530
ends up being there, now
with the Secretary of State

00:57:37.530 --> 00:57:42.530
who is also absolutely
committed to this agenda.

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:45.180
One of the reasons why I
think the department has said

00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:47.460
actually we are not
going to push you harder,

00:57:47.460 --> 00:57:49.510
is because the Secretary of State

00:57:50.669 --> 00:57:54.982
recognises the priority and is determined

00:57:54.982 --> 00:57:58.230
that everything he can
to protect the position.

00:57:58.230 --> 00:58:02.020
'cause he understands
and is really pushing us

00:58:03.330 --> 00:58:06.150
on the importance of this for
the kind of long-term future.

00:58:06.150 --> 00:58:08.039
- [Richard] And transformation.

00:58:08.039 --> 00:58:09.463
- I mean the immediate this winter,

00:58:09.463 --> 00:58:10.620
but actually the long-term
future of the NHS.

00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:15.030
- I agree, he absolutely supports that.

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:15.980
Drives it actually.

00:58:17.834 --> 00:58:19.134
Ruth, you want to.

00:58:19.134 --> 00:58:19.967
- Yeah, thank you.

00:58:19.967 --> 00:58:23.130
So going back then to
maternity funding, Julian,

00:58:23.130 --> 00:58:26.790
we know that many, many of our trusts

00:58:26.790 --> 00:58:29.807
and organisations and
systems are continuing

00:58:29.807 --> 00:58:32.012
to invest in maternity this year,

00:58:32.012 --> 00:58:32.845
which is really good to see,

00:58:32.845 --> 00:58:34.560
but we'll need to make sure that continues

00:58:34.560 --> 00:58:35.850
throughout the rest of the year.

00:58:35.850 --> 00:58:39.600
But it's great to see actually
trust making the decisions

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:43.320
without a national mandate
to continue to invest.

00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:45.240
We just need to make sure that continues.

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:46.503
- Yeah, absolutely Ruth.

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:48.780
And in fact one of the things

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:51.780
that some of the kind of narrow
productivity calculations

00:58:51.780 --> 00:58:54.600
fail to recognise is where we're investing

00:58:54.600 --> 00:58:56.703
to maintain and improve quality,

00:58:57.731 --> 00:58:58.890
which on a kind of a narrow definition

00:58:58.890 --> 00:59:00.310
isn't always caught

00:59:02.096 --> 00:59:03.780
in the sort of productivity calculations.

00:59:03.780 --> 00:59:06.750
- Julian, thank you, as always, a master

00:59:06.750 --> 00:59:10.080
of your brief and very clear,
so thank you very much.

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:13.950
So we're going to move I
think to the winter plan

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:16.360
and David, I think you and Sarah Jane

00:59:17.245 --> 00:59:19.350
and Julian Redhead also joined us

00:59:19.350 --> 00:59:21.120
for this item, and just to reiterate,

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:23.437
I think Amanda said it
and Wol also said it,

00:59:23.437 --> 00:59:26.250
this is the earliest this
this has ever been done

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:28.470
and the letter that goes out today,

00:59:28.470 --> 00:59:29.823
it's a really cogent,

00:59:30.809 --> 00:59:32.610
punchy plan working through our systems,

00:59:32.610 --> 00:59:36.630
working with the ICBs, I
think it's a really good step

00:59:36.630 --> 00:59:38.967
to go this early, David.

00:59:38.967 --> 00:59:40.285
- Thank you, thank you.

00:59:40.285 --> 00:59:41.588
I will almost immediately

00:59:41.588 --> 00:59:42.421
hand over to colleague Sarah Jane

00:59:42.421 --> 00:59:44.220
who's is our national director of urgent

00:59:44.220 --> 00:59:46.470
and emergency care and Dr. Julian Redhead

00:59:46.470 --> 00:59:47.689
who's our national clinical Director

00:59:47.689 --> 00:59:49.698
for urgent and emergency care.

00:59:49.698 --> 00:59:52.680
Just by, I mean I think I'll
say it for the fourth time,

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:55.117
isn't it great to bring this
to a July meeting of the board?

00:59:55.117 --> 00:59:59.910
I would say, and it's very much building

00:59:59.910 --> 01:00:01.380
on work that's been entrained

01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:03.750
for almost two years now
and I would really commend

01:00:03.750 --> 01:00:05.310
the content of the report

01:00:05.310 --> 01:00:07.623
of the content of the plan and report.

01:00:08.848 --> 01:00:10.710
I really do think it
sets us in a good place

01:00:10.710 --> 01:00:12.496
as we enter into the winter months.

01:00:12.496 --> 01:00:15.872
So I will hand over straight
to Sarah you please.

01:00:15.872 --> 01:00:17.182
Sarah Jane

01:00:17.182 --> 01:00:18.015
- Thank you very much David.

01:00:18.015 --> 01:00:18.950
And yeah, I'm just going
to make a few points

01:00:18.950 --> 01:00:20.476
because I know people
would've read the paper,

01:00:20.476 --> 01:00:22.043
then I'm going to hand to Julian

01:00:22.043 --> 01:00:23.430
just to reinforce I think
some of the key messages

01:00:23.430 --> 01:00:26.223
that we want to share
with the board today.

01:00:27.159 --> 01:00:27.992
I think the first thing I want to say

01:00:27.992 --> 01:00:30.000
is that the UEC recovery plan

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:31.912
was published at the end of January,

01:00:31.912 --> 01:00:35.580
was the winter plan way
in advance of winter.

01:00:35.580 --> 01:00:37.710
It was the learning from
what had happened before

01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:40.080
and it was the working with
all of our systems, providers,

01:00:40.080 --> 01:00:43.380
clinical teams on the things
that they want to see change

01:00:43.380 --> 01:00:46.740
so we can improve care
for patients and families.

01:00:46.740 --> 01:00:49.680
So the winter plan builds on
that and the first section

01:00:49.680 --> 01:00:51.926
of the letter, which has
actually now gone out,

01:00:51.926 --> 01:00:53.430
so hopefully colleagues
out there will have had

01:00:53.430 --> 01:00:55.413
an opportunity to read it.

01:00:55.413 --> 01:00:58.410
It's just to pull out the
10 high impact interventions

01:00:58.410 --> 01:01:00.900
in the plan and just really reinforce

01:01:00.900 --> 01:01:01.890
the importance of them.

01:01:01.890 --> 01:01:03.657
There's nothing new.

01:01:03.657 --> 01:01:05.820
You know, as Andrew said,
people have got a lot on,

01:01:05.820 --> 01:01:07.260
they don't want a lot of new stuff.

01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:08.910
This is about strengthening the things

01:01:08.910 --> 01:01:10.230
that we already know work.

01:01:10.230 --> 01:01:11.790
We've already sent out communication

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:13.560
about this a few weeks ago as well,

01:01:13.560 --> 01:01:16.380
sort of co-defined for
people in a bit more detail.

01:01:16.380 --> 01:01:17.550
I would also just add,

01:01:17.550 --> 01:01:20.970
we've been doing some work with
with Julian's team on this,

01:01:20.970 --> 01:01:22.770
the actions that improve quality

01:01:22.770 --> 01:01:25.560
and flow also improve productivity.

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:27.840
So they're the same things
that we are trying to do

01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:30.390
I think to get that flow moving

01:01:30.390 --> 01:01:32.236
as some of the things

01:01:32.236 --> 01:01:33.553
that we just described in the last item.

01:01:33.553 --> 01:01:34.386
So that's the first section.

01:01:34.386 --> 01:01:37.050
The second then is
about asking our systems

01:01:37.050 --> 01:01:39.540
to work together across
all the different parts

01:01:39.540 --> 01:01:41.250
to look at their operational

01:01:41.250 --> 01:01:43.890
and surge plans for this winter.

01:01:43.890 --> 01:01:45.600
They've already done the baseline plans

01:01:45.600 --> 01:01:47.520
as part of the planning round.

01:01:47.520 --> 01:01:51.960
We released the winter money
recurrently early this year.

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:53.490
So people have been able to put

01:01:53.490 --> 01:01:55.290
some of that core capacity in place

01:01:55.290 --> 01:01:58.410
rather than some of the sort
of surge capacity in the past

01:01:58.410 --> 01:02:00.420
where we've opened and
closed beds when we know

01:02:00.420 --> 01:02:02.395
we need to get those
beds open recurrently.

01:02:02.395 --> 01:02:04.920
But we need to go back and
refresh some of those plans now.

01:02:04.920 --> 01:02:07.290
We need to look again at
the demand assumptions

01:02:07.290 --> 01:02:10.080
on different scenarios
and where people are

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:11.520
with those capacity plans.

01:02:11.520 --> 01:02:13.230
And going back to the
question David raised,

01:02:13.230 --> 01:02:16.110
we've also now got the
better care fund plans.

01:02:16.110 --> 01:02:17.520
So we know what capacity and demand

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:19.110
looks like in social care.

01:02:19.110 --> 01:02:22.527
So we're asking people to
lock those two things together

01:02:22.527 --> 01:02:25.200
and work out any gaps and what needs

01:02:25.200 --> 01:02:26.550
to be done by way of surge

01:02:26.550 --> 01:02:28.290
as we go into the winter period.

01:02:28.290 --> 01:02:31.440
We've also today, as part
of the winter letter,

01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:34.530
launched an incentive scheme for trusts

01:02:34.530 --> 01:02:38.310
and that's really for those
places that are doing well

01:02:38.310 --> 01:02:41.100
at the moment but know
that they could stretch

01:02:41.100 --> 01:02:43.950
and achieve more, an
incentivization to improve

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:46.797
on both the A&amp;E target

01:02:46.797 --> 01:02:49.050
that was published as part
of the UEC recovery plan

01:02:49.050 --> 01:02:52.178
and also the focus on ambulance
handovers that we know

01:02:52.178 --> 01:02:54.750
makes a huge difference
to our ability to be able

01:02:54.750 --> 01:02:56.820
to move patients through the system

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:59.630
and get our ambulances
back out on the road.

01:02:59.630 --> 01:03:01.666
The next part then is just the clarity

01:03:01.666 --> 01:03:03.842
around roles and responsibilities.

01:03:03.842 --> 01:03:06.569
This is the first time
that we've really had ICBs

01:03:06.569 --> 01:03:07.868
in full flow.

01:03:07.868 --> 01:03:09.090
I know we've got our ICSs sort
of up and running last year,

01:03:09.090 --> 01:03:10.473
but they're embedded now.

01:03:11.336 --> 01:03:13.050
It's really important
that this is a team sport,

01:03:13.050 --> 01:03:14.725
it's for everybody to be involved in.

01:03:14.725 --> 01:03:17.430
But at the same time
there are specific roles

01:03:17.430 --> 01:03:20.010
and responsibilities for
individual organisations

01:03:20.010 --> 01:03:21.330
within the system

01:03:21.330 --> 01:03:23.640
and it's really important
that everybody does the bit

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:25.800
that they can do as well as the system

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:26.820
as a whole coming together.

01:03:26.820 --> 01:03:30.450
So we've set out the roles
and responsibilities of ICBs

01:03:30.450 --> 01:03:33.330
but also all of the
component parts of the system

01:03:33.330 --> 01:03:35.760
and we are asking boards
to look at those roles

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:38.730
and responsibilities
and to assure themselves

01:03:38.730 --> 01:03:40.290
that they're doing everything they can

01:03:40.290 --> 01:03:43.410
on their bit that adds
up to the collective

01:03:43.410 --> 01:03:44.910
of what we're trying to achieve.

01:03:44.910 --> 01:03:47.190
We're also really pleased
that we've worked closely

01:03:47.190 --> 01:03:48.810
with colleagues in the
Department of Health

01:03:48.810 --> 01:03:52.170
and social care so that a corresponding

01:03:52.170 --> 01:03:55.050
piece of correspondence
will go out from them

01:03:55.050 --> 01:03:57.360
over the course of the next 24 hours,

01:03:57.360 --> 01:04:00.090
which will set out their
approach to what they're asking

01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:04.440
local authorities to do to plan
in the best way for winter.

01:04:04.440 --> 01:04:05.670
And we've worked together on that

01:04:05.670 --> 01:04:07.500
so that we are really describing

01:04:07.500 --> 01:04:10.050
in particular how ICBs
and local authorities

01:04:10.050 --> 01:04:13.963
come together to ensure all
that that capacity is in place.

01:04:13.963 --> 01:04:18.300
There's also a section in
our letter about our people.

01:04:18.300 --> 01:04:20.160
We will only get through the winter

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:23.490
with our people no matter
what plans we put in place.

01:04:23.490 --> 01:04:25.560
It is the goodwill and the hard yards

01:04:25.560 --> 01:04:27.960
that other things that
often make a difference.

01:04:27.960 --> 01:04:31.440
And so there's a significant
section in there just again,

01:04:31.440 --> 01:04:33.480
asking people to go back
to the people promise

01:04:33.480 --> 01:04:35.220
and do the things we
can to look after staff,

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:38.157
like offering vaccinations and so on.

01:04:38.157 --> 01:04:40.680
And then just before I hand
to Julian, one final comment.

01:04:40.680 --> 01:04:43.284
I think there's a lot
at stake this winter,

01:04:43.284 --> 01:04:46.564
last winter was a really difficult period

01:04:46.564 --> 01:04:47.999
for everybody who worked in the NHS

01:04:47.999 --> 01:04:49.170
and more importantly for many people

01:04:49.170 --> 01:04:50.460
who tried to get help from us,

01:04:50.460 --> 01:04:54.450
I think you said it precisely
chair in your opening

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:56.190
when you said what the distress

01:04:56.190 --> 01:04:59.454
that you saw people in and the fact

01:04:59.454 --> 01:05:01.590
that the ambulance crew arrived
on the scene and was able

01:05:01.590 --> 01:05:04.230
to provide that help and support
and the relief it brought.

01:05:04.230 --> 01:05:08.070
And we mustn't leave people
in situations over this winter

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:10.290
where they need the help
you saw with your own eyes

01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:11.910
and we are not able to provide it

01:05:11.910 --> 01:05:14.250
because the ambulance is
stuck outside of a hospital.

01:05:14.250 --> 01:05:18.540
So I think we all share that
endeavour across the people

01:05:18.540 --> 01:05:21.090
who work in the NHS and the
people who use our services.

01:05:21.090 --> 01:05:23.520
So we are 100% committed to
this and we'll move forward

01:05:23.520 --> 01:05:25.200
from here as a team.

01:05:25.200 --> 01:05:27.683
But I think Julian, you're
just going to add a few points.

01:05:29.144 --> 01:05:30.450
- No, thank you Sarah Jane,

01:05:30.450 --> 01:05:31.751
just to build on some of the points

01:05:31.751 --> 01:05:33.066
that you were making really, I mean,

01:05:33.066 --> 01:05:34.378
I think we heard about the issues

01:05:34.378 --> 01:05:35.430
across the UEC being busier.

01:05:35.430 --> 01:05:37.620
The industrial action
affects the UEC pathway.

01:05:37.620 --> 01:05:39.330
It may make some of
the performance figures

01:05:39.330 --> 01:05:40.920
look a little bit better sometimes,

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:42.660
but that doesn't mean the hard work

01:05:42.660 --> 01:05:45.150
that has to go in across the systems

01:05:45.150 --> 01:05:46.830
to make that work as well.

01:05:46.830 --> 01:05:48.480
And obviously we've got capacity issues

01:05:48.480 --> 01:05:50.179
across all parts of our system as well.

01:05:50.179 --> 01:05:54.030
And I think the recovery plan
and this letter for winter,

01:05:54.030 --> 01:05:57.030
the winter plan builds
on those and helps us

01:05:57.030 --> 01:05:59.338
to give some resilience and really to work

01:05:59.338 --> 01:06:00.669
with our our staff.

01:06:00.669 --> 01:06:02.760
I think it's important to remember

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:04.830
that the recovery plan itself

01:06:04.830 --> 01:06:07.680
was built on clinical
involvement and it was based

01:06:07.680 --> 01:06:09.480
on whole lot of clinicians coming together

01:06:09.480 --> 01:06:11.100
to decide how to do this.

01:06:11.100 --> 01:06:13.463
And this plan again delivers on that

01:06:13.463 --> 01:06:15.780
given the 10 high impact interventions,

01:06:15.780 --> 01:06:17.511
which I think most
clinicians would recognise

01:06:17.511 --> 01:06:20.006
and say these are the
high impact interventions

01:06:20.006 --> 01:06:23.850
and builds in some of the points
we made in the board today.

01:06:23.850 --> 01:06:25.260
The single point of access,

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:27.990
talking about where patients can go,

01:06:27.990 --> 01:06:31.021
alternatives to ED for treatment
and indeed alternatives

01:06:31.021 --> 01:06:33.630
from admission as well.

01:06:33.630 --> 01:06:38.040
And the community and mental
health length of stays

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:40.110
and how we work around capacity and length

01:06:40.110 --> 01:06:42.240
of stay across all parts of our system

01:06:42.240 --> 01:06:44.678
are all parts of this
recovery plan as well.

01:06:44.678 --> 01:06:47.043
And the final point is
it builds on the plans

01:06:47.043 --> 01:06:49.290
that we've also had with primary care,

01:06:49.290 --> 01:06:52.320
the elective recovery and
indeed the operational plan

01:06:52.320 --> 01:06:53.550
for the NHS as well.

01:06:53.550 --> 01:06:56.280
So I think all of those
tie together very nicely

01:06:56.280 --> 01:06:58.440
into this winter plan letter.

01:06:58.440 --> 01:06:59.493
- Julian, Sarah Jane,

01:07:00.391 --> 01:07:02.279
David, thank you very
much for that intro, Mark?

01:07:02.279 --> 01:07:04.629
- I want to pick up
something that Julian said,

01:07:06.037 --> 01:07:07.951
Sarah Jane also said,

01:07:07.951 --> 01:07:09.730
which is this is going to be a
winter when we have again flu

01:07:09.730 --> 01:07:11.400
and COVID and that's
not a good combination

01:07:11.400 --> 01:07:12.960
to get at the same time.

01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:14.970
And it sort of turns
on the vaccine question

01:07:14.970 --> 01:07:16.830
which is that's a lot of vaccines

01:07:16.830 --> 01:07:20.340
to get out there and are flu
vaccines and COVID vaccines

01:07:20.340 --> 01:07:21.870
going to be given
simultaneously to people?

01:07:21.870 --> 01:07:23.310
Is that the plan,

01:07:23.310 --> 01:07:25.625
because otherwise it's
quite a demanding programme.

01:07:25.625 --> 01:07:26.883
- To go for that.

01:07:28.414 --> 01:07:29.721
- Yeah.

01:07:29.721 --> 01:07:31.018
- Yeah, thanks Mark.

01:07:31.018 --> 01:07:32.314
So the government is in the final stages

01:07:32.314 --> 01:07:33.644
of considering JCVI's advice
on the autumn campaign.

01:07:33.644 --> 01:07:38.040
So subject to that, our intention

01:07:38.040 --> 01:07:40.380
will be to try and build
on the programme last year

01:07:40.380 --> 01:07:42.030
where we increased co-administration.

01:07:42.030 --> 01:07:45.930
It's not possible to organise
the programme in a flawless

01:07:45.930 --> 01:07:48.300
way, 'cause the supply
arrangements are very different.

01:07:48.300 --> 01:07:51.236
So we manage one centrally
and we don't manage the other

01:07:51.236 --> 01:07:53.400
but there's a number of improvements

01:07:53.400 --> 01:07:54.810
that we've managed to put in place

01:07:54.810 --> 01:07:57.985
with providers and systems
that should make it easier

01:07:57.985 --> 01:08:01.185
and more possible to do
co-administration of flu and COVID

01:08:01.185 --> 01:08:04.986
this campaign and the
experience that people

01:08:04.986 --> 01:08:08.100
who are being invited for vaccination

01:08:08.100 --> 01:08:10.710
will also be better than
last year, so last year,

01:08:10.710 --> 01:08:14.880
you have to book separately on
the national booking service,

01:08:14.880 --> 01:08:16.020
this year you'll be allowed,

01:08:16.020 --> 01:08:18.040
you'll be able to book both vaccines

01:08:19.207 --> 01:08:20.513
at the same time if you wish to do,

01:08:21.404 --> 01:08:23.507
so we'll build on some of
the progress from last year.

01:08:23.507 --> 01:08:25.535
- It, oh that's important

01:08:25.535 --> 01:08:26.959
'cause it will increase compliance.

01:08:26.959 --> 01:08:27.960
It's much easier to go once than twice.

01:08:27.960 --> 01:08:29.587
- Indeed.

01:08:29.587 --> 01:08:30.840
- And I mean on the healthcare
worker issue as well,

01:08:30.840 --> 01:08:33.540
it's really important
that that's the protection

01:08:33.540 --> 01:08:35.280
the workforce needs.

01:08:35.280 --> 01:08:37.080
And so I mean are there
going to be measures

01:08:37.080 --> 01:08:40.820
to try and ensure as far as possible

01:08:40.820 --> 01:08:42.330
that healthcare providers make sure

01:08:42.330 --> 01:08:45.210
that as far as possible,
healthcare staff are vaccinated?

01:08:45.210 --> 01:08:47.031
- Oh gosh, absolutely.

01:08:47.031 --> 01:08:48.345
And it's a standard that we always call

01:08:48.345 --> 01:08:51.237
very close to and yes, absolutely.

01:08:51.237 --> 01:08:52.920
- I remember historically
my time in medicine,

01:08:52.920 --> 01:08:55.020
it wasn't necessarily the
uptake wasn't as as good

01:08:55.020 --> 01:08:57.810
as one would like, it's
probably better now.

01:08:57.810 --> 01:09:00.300
- Yeah, but I mean
everything, yes, absolutely,

01:09:00.300 --> 01:09:01.456
that will be the plan.

01:09:01.456 --> 01:09:02.880
- Tim, you want to come in?

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:03.713
I think.

01:09:04.614 --> 01:09:05.914
- Thank you chair.

01:09:05.914 --> 01:09:06.840
I just wanted to point out an observation

01:09:06.840 --> 01:09:09.554
and maybe Sarah Jane, Julian,

01:09:09.554 --> 01:09:11.910
tell me if I've got this wrong,

01:09:11.910 --> 01:09:14.550
but my impression is over
the past several years

01:09:14.550 --> 01:09:16.765
that as we go into this winter,

01:09:16.765 --> 01:09:21.765
the amount of data at
every level of the system

01:09:21.930 --> 01:09:25.500
in real time is at an all time high.

01:09:25.500 --> 01:09:28.392
Like we are in a very different place

01:09:28.392 --> 01:09:30.120
with the management of data

01:09:30.120 --> 01:09:34.260
so that we are seeing at
the frontline, the managers

01:09:34.260 --> 01:09:37.710
at the frontline have not just their data

01:09:37.710 --> 01:09:40.144
but comparative data on performance.

01:09:40.144 --> 01:09:45.144
The ICBs now have quite a
bit of data and dashboards

01:09:46.020 --> 01:09:49.440
and at the level of the national system,

01:09:49.440 --> 01:09:53.040
there are many more metrics
now that we are looking at

01:09:53.040 --> 01:09:57.120
regularly in Amanda's performance,

01:09:57.120 --> 01:09:58.470
weekly performance meetings.

01:09:58.470 --> 01:10:00.175
And it just seems to me,

01:10:00.175 --> 01:10:02.830
that's a materially different place

01:10:03.747 --> 01:10:06.960
that we're in, both the speed
and the extent of the metrics.

01:10:06.960 --> 01:10:10.050
And it hasn't been commented on here

01:10:10.050 --> 01:10:13.648
and I just thought it was
useful to call that out.

01:10:13.648 --> 01:10:14.523
- Very good.

01:10:15.390 --> 01:10:17.430
Any other comments or reflections?

01:10:17.430 --> 01:10:19.050
Amanda, do you want to comment?

01:10:19.050 --> 01:10:22.530
You hosted yesterday with
David a call for chairs

01:10:22.530 --> 01:10:25.710
and CEOs of trusts and systems

01:10:25.710 --> 01:10:27.030
and actually chief medical officers

01:10:27.030 --> 01:10:28.320
and chief nurses around the system,

01:10:28.320 --> 01:10:31.370
which was hugely well attended in advance

01:10:31.370 --> 01:10:33.616
of actually the letter going out today.

01:10:33.616 --> 01:10:34.680
I think that was a very good idea

01:10:34.680 --> 01:10:36.600
just a forewarn and have a conversation

01:10:36.600 --> 01:10:39.450
about sort of what was going to be coming,

01:10:39.450 --> 01:10:41.970
the next day IE today,

01:10:41.970 --> 01:10:43.295
any further comments you want to make on

01:10:43.295 --> 01:10:45.340
the winter planning?

01:10:45.340 --> 01:10:47.760
- I mean I'll let David
have the final word

01:10:47.760 --> 01:10:51.030
but I think we've covered
it pretty comprehensively

01:10:51.030 --> 01:10:52.740
and we obviously had a
chance to talk about it

01:10:52.740 --> 01:10:54.840
as a board before,

01:10:54.840 --> 01:10:57.330
but certainly my sense
is that actually the fact

01:10:57.330 --> 01:11:01.200
that this is very explicitly building on

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:03.300
the things that were outlined

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:04.860
in the urgent emergency
care recovery plan,

01:11:04.860 --> 01:11:07.050
which in turn built on the things

01:11:07.050 --> 01:11:11.070
that we did put in place over last winter,

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:13.350
which in turn built on
the joint work that we did

01:11:13.350 --> 01:11:15.420
with system colleagues to come up

01:11:15.420 --> 01:11:18.660
with that plan means
that A, I think genuinely

01:11:18.660 --> 01:11:20.580
there are no surprises in this

01:11:20.580 --> 01:11:22.750
and that's as it kind of should be.

01:11:22.750 --> 01:11:26.753
But what there is is now a
whole suite of resources,

01:11:26.753 --> 01:11:29.370
which is partly the
commitment that we made.

01:11:29.370 --> 01:11:30.720
What can we do nationally?

01:11:30.720 --> 01:11:34.080
And part of what we can do
nationally is be very clear

01:11:34.080 --> 01:11:36.820
to Tim's point what the data tells us,

01:11:36.820 --> 01:11:39.060
what the evidence base is,

01:11:39.060 --> 01:11:42.360
collate those examples of
where things have been put

01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:44.130
into practise and really
made a difference.

01:11:44.130 --> 01:11:46.170
And then really support colleagues

01:11:46.170 --> 01:11:47.970
using much more of an
improvement approach,

01:11:47.970 --> 01:11:50.220
which is very much what
Sarah Jane has been leading

01:11:50.220 --> 01:11:53.400
with Vin and others to then help

01:11:53.400 --> 01:11:54.930
support colleagues and systems

01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:56.310
and in individual organisations

01:11:56.310 --> 01:11:59.130
put that into place locally.

01:11:59.130 --> 01:12:01.569
But you know, there is
a sharp edge to this.

01:12:01.569 --> 01:12:05.610
This is about us recognising
there is a absolute commitment

01:12:05.610 --> 01:12:08.762
we all share to doing our
absolute best for patients,

01:12:08.762 --> 01:12:10.980
supporting our staff,

01:12:10.980 --> 01:12:13.440
but ultimately it's always patient first

01:12:13.440 --> 01:12:15.660
and over winter,

01:12:15.660 --> 01:12:20.070
we do see really Mark's made
exactly the right point.

01:12:20.070 --> 01:12:23.790
We do see occasionally some
really significant demand

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:27.240
and it is our job to make
sure we have done everything

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:30.300
we possibly can to get ready in advance

01:12:30.300 --> 01:12:33.930
so that we're as resilient as
we can be heading into winter.

01:12:33.930 --> 01:12:35.864
Yeah, so I think we've talked

01:12:35.864 --> 01:12:36.840
about the context of industrial action.

01:12:36.840 --> 01:12:39.510
I very much hope we will not be in a place

01:12:39.510 --> 01:12:41.835
where we're continuing
to see industrial action

01:12:41.835 --> 01:12:43.352
over the winter.

01:12:43.352 --> 01:12:48.352
We would continue to urge all
colleagues to work together

01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:52.710
to try and make sure that
we are seeking resolution

01:12:52.710 --> 01:12:55.830
but we have to be prepared and equally,

01:12:55.830 --> 01:12:57.450
we recognise some of
the financial pressures

01:12:57.450 --> 01:13:00.420
but that's part of the
reason why we were so clear

01:13:00.420 --> 01:13:02.850
about getting the plans out
early so that colleagues

01:13:02.850 --> 01:13:05.730
could plan on that basis
and why it is so welcome

01:13:05.730 --> 01:13:07.830
that we've got the joint work
with social care colleagues

01:13:07.830 --> 01:13:10.080
and with other partners
so that we can make sure

01:13:10.080 --> 01:13:12.300
we're as joined up as we possibly can be.

01:13:12.300 --> 01:13:14.940
So this feels like an important moment,

01:13:14.940 --> 01:13:17.547
but it also felt to me talking
to colleagues across the NHS

01:13:17.547 --> 01:13:21.480
and the feedback that we've
had that it's welcome clarity

01:13:21.480 --> 01:13:23.080
and people are really up for it.

01:13:24.142 --> 01:13:24.975
- Thank you, Andrew.

01:13:24.975 --> 01:13:26.940
So thanks Richard, so asked I myself,

01:13:26.940 --> 01:13:28.072
well what's different to last year

01:13:28.072 --> 01:13:31.530
and I think a number of things different.

01:13:31.530 --> 01:13:34.920
A, we've got an accountability
framework that we didn't have

01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:38.190
last year, which I think
is a big step forward.

01:13:38.190 --> 01:13:41.490
B, a lot of this can only
be done at system level.

01:13:41.490 --> 01:13:44.820
We cannot do this universally

01:13:44.820 --> 01:13:47.250
across the whole of the NHS in England.

01:13:47.250 --> 01:13:49.077
It's not in our gift to do that.

01:13:49.077 --> 01:13:51.720
And I think the focus on system working

01:13:51.720 --> 01:13:53.670
is absolutely the right one.

01:13:53.670 --> 01:13:57.390
The third thing is around
if everyone adopts,

01:13:57.390 --> 01:14:00.810
every system adopts these
10 high impact changes,

01:14:00.810 --> 01:14:02.520
we will see a change.

01:14:02.520 --> 01:14:07.520
So it's incumbent on us to
ensure that this actually happens

01:14:07.710 --> 01:14:09.832
because I think if you
apply all of these 10,

01:14:09.832 --> 01:14:12.270
we will see the big step change

01:14:12.270 --> 01:14:15.420
that we want to see and
we're now expecting.

01:14:15.420 --> 01:14:19.615
So I think you need to get into
the kind of monitoring stage

01:14:19.615 --> 01:14:23.886
and supporting systems who
are finding it quite difficult

01:14:23.886 --> 01:14:27.330
to cope with some of this to get them

01:14:27.330 --> 01:14:29.100
in the place where come October,

01:14:29.100 --> 01:14:31.890
they're absolutely ready and raring to go

01:14:31.890 --> 01:14:34.650
and then I think we'll
see a big step change.

01:14:34.650 --> 01:14:36.217
So thank you.

01:14:36.217 --> 01:14:37.524
It's really good.

01:14:37.524 --> 01:14:38.852
- David, last comment.

01:14:38.852 --> 01:14:40.977
I think what's been really
positive about the conversation

01:14:40.977 --> 01:14:43.050
isn't it, we've heard from
Julian that this will land well

01:14:43.050 --> 01:14:46.286
with the clinical frontline
and when the chips sit down

01:14:46.286 --> 01:14:47.119
it is about the clinical frontline

01:14:47.119 --> 01:14:49.890
and clinical leadership that
will take us to anywhere,

01:14:49.890 --> 01:14:51.564
actually secondly, we've heard yesterday

01:14:51.564 --> 01:14:53.040
this has landed well

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:55.050
with the senior leadership
and management community

01:14:55.050 --> 01:14:56.890
in a very, very well attended webinar.

01:14:56.890 --> 01:14:59.460
And thirdly, I think the reason is this,

01:14:59.460 --> 01:15:01.710
this is a really good sensible plan.

01:15:01.710 --> 01:15:03.153
This is not initiativitis.

01:15:03.153 --> 01:15:06.450
Yeah this builds on what we
know that was every winter plan

01:15:06.450 --> 01:15:07.980
should be better than the last winter plan

01:15:07.980 --> 01:15:10.470
because you should learn
and iterate and move on.

01:15:10.470 --> 01:15:12.150
Therefore this is the best winter plan

01:15:12.150 --> 01:15:13.110
because we have learned,

01:15:13.110 --> 01:15:15.261
iterated and moved on the
key as you say now Andrew

01:15:15.261 --> 01:15:19.050
is going to be about absolute
focus on implementation

01:15:19.050 --> 01:15:20.610
and I thank Sarah Jane actually,

01:15:20.610 --> 01:15:22.470
really, really excellent
leadership actually

01:15:22.470 --> 01:15:24.120
to get us where we've got to today.

01:15:24.120 --> 01:15:27.414
- Very good, so thank you
David, Sarah Jane, Julian,

01:15:27.414 --> 01:15:29.118
thank you very much for that.

01:15:29.118 --> 01:15:31.083
So shall we, let's move on.

01:15:31.920 --> 01:15:34.470
Next item actually it's Chris,

01:15:34.470 --> 01:15:36.291
I think you are leading

01:15:36.291 --> 01:15:37.530
but actually I'm delighted
that we've been joined

01:15:37.530 --> 01:15:42.030
by Dr. Habib Naqvi and
also Dr. Bola Owolabi,

01:15:42.030 --> 01:15:43.966
thank you very much for joining us

01:15:43.966 --> 01:15:45.630
and it's the annual report

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:48.960
on NHS England's work on
healthcare inequalities,

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:51.690
actually including the
work commissioned from

01:15:51.690 --> 01:15:54.780
and delivered by the NHS
Race and Health Observatory.

01:15:54.780 --> 01:15:56.310
So delighted you're both here with us.

01:15:56.310 --> 01:15:57.450
But Chris, do you want to say anything

01:15:57.450 --> 01:15:59.392
by way of introduction?

01:15:59.392 --> 01:16:00.707
- Yeah, so Richard,

01:16:00.707 --> 01:16:02.820
a principle and a statistic
to frame the conversation.

01:16:02.820 --> 01:16:04.650
The principle is just to remind everybody,

01:16:04.650 --> 01:16:06.660
if you go back to the NHS constitution,

01:16:06.660 --> 01:16:10.229
the first principle says the
NHS has a wider social duty

01:16:10.229 --> 01:16:12.990
to promote equality through
the services we provide

01:16:12.990 --> 01:16:15.048
and to pay particular attention to groups

01:16:15.048 --> 01:16:16.643
or sections of society

01:16:16.643 --> 01:16:19.470
where improvements in
health and life expectancy

01:16:19.470 --> 01:16:22.259
are not keeping pace with
the rest of the population.

01:16:22.259 --> 01:16:24.540
And I just think
grounding the conversation

01:16:24.540 --> 01:16:27.105
in that kind of first principle
feels really important.

01:16:27.105 --> 01:16:30.270
What does that kind of
actually mean in practise?

01:16:30.270 --> 01:16:33.125
Well, let me just give
you a statistic and again,

01:16:33.125 --> 01:16:34.438
when you look at some of the statistics

01:16:34.438 --> 01:16:35.750
around health inequalities,

01:16:35.750 --> 01:16:36.810
I just still find them deeply shocking.

01:16:36.810 --> 01:16:39.840
On average, a 60 year old woman

01:16:39.840 --> 01:16:44.220
in the poorest area of
England has diagnosed illness

01:16:44.220 --> 01:16:47.730
equivalent to that of a 76 year old woman

01:16:47.730 --> 01:16:51.690
in the wealthiest area
and it's that 16 year gap

01:16:51.690 --> 01:16:54.840
that lies at the heart of that social duty

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:57.060
to tackle health inequalities.

01:16:57.060 --> 01:17:00.180
So let's hand over to Bola and Habib

01:17:00.180 --> 01:17:02.030
to say how we're actually doing that.

01:17:03.330 --> 01:17:04.530
- Thank you so much Chris.

01:17:04.530 --> 01:17:06.510
I think that's a really important framing

01:17:06.510 --> 01:17:08.580
and my thanks too to the board

01:17:08.580 --> 01:17:10.830
for their support over the
last two and a half years

01:17:10.830 --> 01:17:13.110
actually of the programme.

01:17:13.110 --> 01:17:13.990
I'll just set out

01:17:16.082 --> 01:17:18.449
a number of dimensions of
action that we've taken,

01:17:18.449 --> 01:17:20.730
perhaps talk you through some examples

01:17:20.730 --> 01:17:23.070
of where this policy
is actually translating

01:17:23.070 --> 01:17:26.850
into tangible impact in people's lives

01:17:26.850 --> 01:17:28.170
across the country.

01:17:28.170 --> 01:17:31.140
And then give Habib an
opportunity to then talk

01:17:31.140 --> 01:17:33.480
about how that is playing out from a race

01:17:33.480 --> 01:17:35.820
and health observatory's point of view.

01:17:35.820 --> 01:17:38.460
So I will assume the paper as read,

01:17:38.460 --> 01:17:40.558
but just to say that
the dimensions of action

01:17:40.558 --> 01:17:44.340
are as follows, the five
strategic priorities

01:17:44.340 --> 01:17:45.180
that we've set out,

01:17:45.180 --> 01:17:47.520
we will continue to deliver on those

01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:51.390
including the inclusive
and equitable recovery

01:17:51.390 --> 01:17:54.387
across elective services,
primary care access,

01:17:54.387 --> 01:17:56.968
urgent and emergency care as
we've just been discussing.

01:17:56.968 --> 01:18:00.270
And also just to talk about the model

01:18:00.270 --> 01:18:01.770
for change that we've created,

01:18:01.770 --> 01:18:03.780
the Core20PLUS5 approach

01:18:03.780 --> 01:18:05.970
and indeed the delivery ecosystem

01:18:05.970 --> 01:18:07.380
that we've been able to build

01:18:07.380 --> 01:18:10.590
around that, looking into cancer,

01:18:10.590 --> 01:18:13.110
respiratory disease,
cardiovascular disease,

01:18:13.110 --> 01:18:16.410
mental health, maternity,
oral health, asthma,

01:18:16.410 --> 01:18:18.011
diabetes and epilepsy.

01:18:18.011 --> 01:18:22.680
Just to bring a degree of
focus in terms of the areas

01:18:22.680 --> 01:18:24.390
that we need to accelerate progress on.

01:18:24.390 --> 01:18:26.580
Just picking up on Chris's point there

01:18:26.580 --> 01:18:28.740
around the life expectancy

01:18:28.740 --> 01:18:32.340
and healthy life expectancy
gap and the particular drivers

01:18:32.340 --> 01:18:35.220
of that gap and also the policy influence

01:18:35.220 --> 01:18:37.890
across government and
other arms lengths bodies.

01:18:37.890 --> 01:18:39.780
We've just called out the fact that the UK

01:18:39.780 --> 01:18:42.060
health security agency have adopted

01:18:42.060 --> 01:18:45.240
the Core20PLUS5 approach for describing

01:18:45.240 --> 01:18:46.770
how they're going to do their work

01:18:46.770 --> 01:18:49.414
on health equity going forward.

01:18:49.414 --> 01:18:53.130
Also in terms of the government's
major condition strategy

01:18:53.130 --> 01:18:56.070
and the deep engagement we've
had as that's been developed,

01:18:56.070 --> 01:18:58.050
just to make sure again
that there is fidelity

01:18:58.050 --> 01:19:01.290
and consistency with
the way that NHS England

01:19:01.290 --> 01:19:04.620
is trying to not only provide
that universal support

01:19:04.620 --> 01:19:07.602
but remembering and being
mindful of those other margins

01:19:07.602 --> 01:19:11.040
for whom universal offers
will simply not work.

01:19:11.040 --> 01:19:14.490
And also just to talk you
through the planned deliverables

01:19:14.490 --> 01:19:16.470
over the next financial year,

01:19:16.470 --> 01:19:18.660
in particular drawing your attention

01:19:18.660 --> 01:19:22.557
to the metrics and the indicators
for monitoring progress.

01:19:22.557 --> 01:19:25.170
Because we are mindful that updates

01:19:25.170 --> 01:19:27.390
have been narrative over a period of time.

01:19:27.390 --> 01:19:29.760
We really want to make sure
that in the coming period,

01:19:29.760 --> 01:19:32.640
we are able to demonstrate by the data,

01:19:32.640 --> 01:19:34.816
the narrowing of the gap over time.

01:19:34.816 --> 01:19:37.410
Also the NHS England Commission

01:19:37.410 --> 01:19:40.140
to the Race and Health
Observatory and David's point

01:19:40.140 --> 01:19:41.610
earlier on was very well made

01:19:41.610 --> 01:19:45.246
around that shocking statistic
that was published this week.

01:19:45.246 --> 01:19:49.770
As a matter of fact that was
the learning from the deaths

01:19:49.770 --> 01:19:52.290
of people with learning disabilities

01:19:52.290 --> 01:19:53.790
that came to NHS England's

01:19:53.790 --> 01:19:57.510
Health Inequalities
Improvement Board two years ago

01:19:57.510 --> 01:20:00.960
and seeing that report and year
on year seeing that report,

01:20:00.960 --> 01:20:04.080
we referred to the NHS
Race and Health Observatory

01:20:04.080 --> 01:20:07.370
to support NHS England's
Learning Disability Programme

01:20:07.370 --> 01:20:10.860
in terms of how do we
translate the leader report

01:20:10.860 --> 01:20:12.646
into something meaningful and tangible.

01:20:12.646 --> 01:20:16.590
And I'm sure Habib will speak
more to that in just a moment.

01:20:16.590 --> 01:20:18.780
And just really some examples then

01:20:18.780 --> 01:20:21.690
about what does this mean in practise?

01:20:21.690 --> 01:20:23.340
I just want to say a huge thank you

01:20:23.340 --> 01:20:25.170
to Leeds Teaching Hospitals

01:20:25.170 --> 01:20:28.650
who hosted me not very long
ago and were able to show

01:20:28.650 --> 01:20:30.354
in their cancer pathway,

01:20:30.354 --> 01:20:32.580
looking across the cancer pathway

01:20:32.580 --> 01:20:35.010
that there were significant inequalities

01:20:35.010 --> 01:20:38.850
in terms of the two week
weight breast cancer pathway

01:20:38.850 --> 01:20:42.270
people's tendency to not
attend the appointment

01:20:42.270 --> 01:20:43.702
or late cancellations.

01:20:43.702 --> 01:20:45.900
And when they disaggregated the data,

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:47.790
they found that it was principally people

01:20:47.790 --> 01:20:49.980
from our ethnic minority communities

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:50.813
and people

01:20:50.813 --> 01:20:52.980
from our most socioeconomically
deprived communities

01:20:52.980 --> 01:20:54.192
that tended not to attend

01:20:54.192 --> 01:20:58.380
very important two week wait actually.

01:20:58.380 --> 01:21:02.190
So these are the cancer,
suspected cancer pathways.

01:21:02.190 --> 01:21:05.070
And what they did was
actually to implement

01:21:05.070 --> 01:21:07.140
a very focused intervention,

01:21:07.140 --> 01:21:09.120
telephone calls, reaching out to people,

01:21:09.120 --> 01:21:11.970
understanding the reasons
for non-attendance.

01:21:11.970 --> 01:21:15.946
And with that they reduced
the non-attendance from 16%

01:21:15.946 --> 01:21:20.163
to 3%, through that
focused work that they did.

01:21:20.163 --> 01:21:22.320
Also just want to say a huge thank you

01:21:22.320 --> 01:21:23.793
to our lung cancer programme

01:21:23.793 --> 01:21:26.310
with whom we've been working very closely.

01:21:26.310 --> 01:21:28.770
And again, you'll see that the rate

01:21:28.770 --> 01:21:30.660
of diagnosing cancer in stage one

01:21:30.660 --> 01:21:33.720
and two has significantly
increased overall,

01:21:33.720 --> 01:21:37.530
but in particular for the
20% most deprived communities

01:21:37.530 --> 01:21:39.690
that we refer to as the Core20.

01:21:39.690 --> 01:21:42.420
And that's again a specific example

01:21:42.420 --> 01:21:45.463
of how focused intervention
is making a difference.

01:21:45.463 --> 01:21:48.270
Just thinking about
urgent and emergency care,

01:21:48.270 --> 01:21:51.238
I just want to call out
some work again in Leeds

01:21:51.238 --> 01:21:53.984
working with an organisation called Backer

01:21:53.984 --> 01:21:57.510
to roll out the high intensity use service

01:21:57.510 --> 01:22:02.490
and for 121 highest intensity users

01:22:02.490 --> 01:22:04.290
of accident and emergency,

01:22:04.290 --> 01:22:08.837
they've been able to reduce
A&amp;E attendance by 62%,

01:22:08.837 --> 01:22:13.837
ambulance conveyances by 61%,
emergency admissions by 69%.

01:22:14.670 --> 01:22:16.332
And those changes were sustained

01:22:16.332 --> 01:22:19.770
beyond the intervention period.

01:22:19.770 --> 01:22:22.380
And actually that's then
translated into a saving

01:22:22.380 --> 01:22:27.380
of about 4,456 hours
in emergency department

01:22:28.050 --> 01:22:31.560
through that focused work in
terms of high intensity use,

01:22:31.560 --> 01:22:33.870
just really quickly from
a primary care access

01:22:33.870 --> 01:22:36.450
point of view, I spent the day in Slough

01:22:36.450 --> 01:22:38.100
with Dr. Priya Kumar

01:22:38.100 --> 01:22:39.480
who was able to show

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:42.505
with a multi-generational household pilot,

01:22:42.505 --> 01:22:45.240
we spent an hour in a particular household

01:22:45.240 --> 01:22:47.430
where we were able to do their diabetes

01:22:47.430 --> 01:22:49.830
annual health check, booked in two members

01:22:49.830 --> 01:22:52.560
of the household for their
cervical cancer screening,

01:22:52.560 --> 01:22:56.010
administered the flu vaccine
to the two year old in the home

01:22:56.010 --> 01:22:58.470
and diagnosed the dad with pre-diabetes,

01:22:58.470 --> 01:23:02.190
six members of the household
in an hour consultation,

01:23:02.190 --> 01:23:05.010
just demonstrating the
productivity that we can achieve

01:23:05.010 --> 01:23:06.930
through some of these interventions.

01:23:06.930 --> 01:23:09.120
And I also want to shout out Dr. Beran

01:23:09.120 --> 01:23:11.790
and partners from Frimley ICB,

01:23:11.790 --> 01:23:13.830
who again were able to demonstrate

01:23:13.830 --> 01:23:16.830
from a cardiovascular disease
prevention point of view,

01:23:16.830 --> 01:23:19.140
four times the rate of blood pressure,

01:23:19.140 --> 01:23:20.670
new blood pressure diagnosis

01:23:20.670 --> 01:23:22.890
in their most deprived communities

01:23:22.890 --> 01:23:27.060
compared to the ICS average,
48% that they achieved

01:23:27.060 --> 01:23:30.540
in that practise compared
to 11% in the ICS.

01:23:30.540 --> 01:23:34.200
And they did this through particular focus

01:23:34.200 --> 01:23:36.450
of digital interventions

01:23:36.450 --> 01:23:40.230
for that Core20PLUS
population group and also,

01:23:40.230 --> 01:23:44.010
not just in terms of new
diagnosis but optimal management.

01:23:44.010 --> 01:23:47.670
So five times more patients
in Dr. Beran's practise

01:23:47.670 --> 01:23:49.710
with controlled hypertension,

01:23:49.710 --> 01:23:52.530
again compared to the ICS average.

01:23:52.530 --> 01:23:55.380
And just really from an
innovation point of view,

01:23:55.380 --> 01:23:58.350
working with the Accelerating
Access Collaborative,

01:23:58.350 --> 01:24:01.530
we were able to invest
nearly £4 million last year.

01:24:01.530 --> 01:24:05.810
And I just want to call out
the increase in Inclisiran,

01:24:05.810 --> 01:24:06.750
which is a lipid lowering drug.

01:24:06.750 --> 01:24:08.160
And we had some challenges,

01:24:08.160 --> 01:24:09.630
which I'm sure Tim will remember,

01:24:09.630 --> 01:24:11.310
we had many a conversation about this.

01:24:11.310 --> 01:24:14.302
But by focusing on that Core20PLUS

01:24:14.302 --> 01:24:16.500
with dedicated investment

01:24:16.500 --> 01:24:19.200
through the academic
health science networks,

01:24:19.200 --> 01:24:21.894
they hugely increased
the uptake of Inclisiran.

01:24:21.894 --> 01:24:23.955
But actually for me what's more important

01:24:23.955 --> 01:24:26.910
is what that means for
reduction of strokes

01:24:26.910 --> 01:24:30.180
and heart attacks in the most
vulnerable population groups.

01:24:30.180 --> 01:24:31.440
Lots of other examples,

01:24:31.440 --> 01:24:33.390
but I want to give Habib an opportunity

01:24:33.390 --> 01:24:35.250
to talk about what the RHO

01:24:35.250 --> 01:24:38.880
have been doing and then very
happy to share my three pages

01:24:38.880 --> 01:24:43.230
of absolutely fantastic
work that people are doing

01:24:43.230 --> 01:24:47.055
across the country, translating
policy into practise.

01:24:47.055 --> 01:24:48.497
- Thank you.

01:24:48.497 --> 01:24:50.190
- Thanks Chris and thanks Bola.

01:24:50.190 --> 01:24:55.190
So the observatory is 27
months into its work programme

01:24:55.320 --> 01:24:58.140
and its mission to ensure racial equity

01:24:58.140 --> 01:25:02.340
in health and care system.

01:25:02.340 --> 01:25:04.650
And we do that through kind of three legs

01:25:04.650 --> 01:25:06.030
of our operating model,

01:25:06.030 --> 01:25:09.584
bringing together the
evidence base where it exists

01:25:09.584 --> 01:25:11.010
or establishing it where it doesn't exist.

01:25:11.010 --> 01:25:12.180
Secondly, turning that evidence

01:25:12.180 --> 01:25:16.020
into policy recommendations
for change and thirdly,

01:25:16.020 --> 01:25:18.840
supporting the system to
implement those recommendations

01:25:18.840 --> 01:25:21.330
and racial equity more
generally on the ground.

01:25:21.330 --> 01:25:24.105
And I'll talk very briefly
on those three points.

01:25:24.105 --> 01:25:25.650
On research and evidence,

01:25:25.650 --> 01:25:28.500
we're continuing to generate
and mobilise evidence

01:25:28.500 --> 01:25:30.957
to support the reduction
of ethnic inequalities.

01:25:30.957 --> 01:25:34.080
I'll just talk about two examples of that.

01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:37.108
Two groundbreaking reviews
that we published this month.

01:25:37.108 --> 01:25:41.600
Firstly the neonatal testing review,

01:25:41.600 --> 01:25:44.460
which was launched at the House of Commons

01:25:44.460 --> 01:25:45.780
earlier this month,

01:25:45.780 --> 01:25:49.260
receiving cross party support
and leading to commitments

01:25:49.260 --> 01:25:52.251
in terms of action, in terms of de-biasing

01:25:52.251 --> 01:25:55.859
some of the issues that
we see within testing

01:25:55.859 --> 01:25:59.322
testing and APGAR score
testing in particular,

01:25:59.322 --> 01:26:03.540
and commitments for
action from NICE, NIHR,

01:26:03.540 --> 01:26:08.540
the CQC and NMC who all
attended that particular launch.

01:26:08.760 --> 01:26:12.010
And the second report, which
we published on Tuesday

01:26:13.355 --> 01:26:18.330
on a well-attended webinar that I chaired

01:26:18.330 --> 01:26:21.370
that also included people
with learning disabilities

01:26:22.860 --> 01:26:24.720
that were part of the working group,

01:26:24.720 --> 01:26:27.318
including the researchers of that work.

01:26:27.318 --> 01:26:31.140
And also Tom Cahill from NHS England,

01:26:31.140 --> 01:26:34.200
which is the statistic
David that you highlighted,

01:26:34.200 --> 01:26:36.490
the shocking figure of life expectancy

01:26:37.347 --> 01:26:38.820
being half that ethnic minority

01:26:38.820 --> 01:26:40.928
people with a learning disability,

01:26:40.928 --> 01:26:43.830
men with learning disability
compared to white men.

01:26:43.830 --> 01:26:45.780
And as with all of our work,

01:26:45.780 --> 01:26:48.480
we will be taking those
recommendations forward

01:26:48.480 --> 01:26:50.520
and working with those organisations

01:26:50.520 --> 01:26:53.010
that are cited within the recommendations

01:26:53.010 --> 01:26:55.530
to make sure that we have
action on the ground.

01:26:55.530 --> 01:26:58.740
And in fact that piece of
work reminds me of two points,

01:26:58.740 --> 01:27:02.250
I guess, one is that our
work isn't just about race,

01:27:02.250 --> 01:27:03.900
it's about saving lives

01:27:03.900 --> 01:27:06.540
and it's about efficiency
within the system.

01:27:06.540 --> 01:27:11.540
And secondly that it's a
good example of how our model

01:27:11.970 --> 01:27:14.760
of holding up a mirror
to the rest of the system

01:27:14.760 --> 01:27:15.960
and then supporting it

01:27:15.960 --> 01:27:18.600
in order to see improvements and to focus

01:27:18.600 --> 01:27:21.426
on those actionable recommendations

01:27:21.426 --> 01:27:25.593
is something that is beginning to work.

01:27:26.760 --> 01:27:29.010
The second point is around partnerships

01:27:29.010 --> 01:27:30.750
and we continue to have close partnerships

01:27:30.750 --> 01:27:33.990
with ALBs, with the government,

01:27:33.990 --> 01:27:36.210
Bola talked about the
major condition strategy,

01:27:36.210 --> 01:27:37.560
so we're inputting into that,

01:27:37.560 --> 01:27:39.510
but also with the other parties as well

01:27:39.510 --> 01:27:41.820
and with the shadow party as well

01:27:41.820 --> 01:27:44.730
in terms of their manifestos

01:27:44.730 --> 01:27:48.090
around health and race as
we move forward as well.

01:27:48.090 --> 01:27:51.540
We've signed a partnership
statement with the NIHR,

01:27:51.540 --> 01:27:53.880
which formalises our influence

01:27:53.880 --> 01:27:57.330
in terms of research, not
just what is looked at,

01:27:57.330 --> 01:27:59.580
but who leads the research as well,

01:27:59.580 --> 01:28:04.440
as well as a agreement with the CQC,

01:28:04.440 --> 01:28:07.135
looking at their state of care report

01:28:07.135 --> 01:28:10.681
and how our influence
can help them to reflect

01:28:10.681 --> 01:28:12.750
ethnic minority communities,

01:28:12.750 --> 01:28:15.152
particularly in relation
to long-term conditions.

01:28:15.152 --> 01:28:17.940
But we're not limiting our knowledge pool

01:28:17.940 --> 01:28:19.620
within our own geographical borders.

01:28:19.620 --> 01:28:21.390
And my vision for the observatory

01:28:21.390 --> 01:28:24.270
right from the outset was
for it to be a global player

01:28:24.270 --> 01:28:27.060
in this space and
therefore our relationship

01:28:27.060 --> 01:28:30.300
with the CDC in the US
is progressing well.

01:28:30.300 --> 01:28:33.870
And that was on the back of
the previous G seven summit.

01:28:33.870 --> 01:28:37.095
So we're putting together
a very comprehensive

01:28:37.095 --> 01:28:37.950
work programme

01:28:37.950 --> 01:28:40.920
with the US around some of these key areas

01:28:40.920 --> 01:28:45.090
around racial equity as
well as our relationship

01:28:45.090 --> 01:28:47.730
with the Commonwealth Fund

01:28:47.730 --> 01:28:52.730
and our established
International Race Experts group,

01:28:53.670 --> 01:28:57.960
which is co-chaired by Yvonne Coghill

01:28:57.960 --> 01:29:01.080
and Professor David Williams
from Harvard University,

01:29:01.080 --> 01:29:05.461
bringing race and health
experts from New Zealand,

01:29:05.461 --> 01:29:07.740
Australia, South Africa, Guatemala,

01:29:07.740 --> 01:29:09.300
the US and Canada who joined us

01:29:09.300 --> 01:29:13.490
in the international
conference we had last year.

01:29:13.490 --> 01:29:16.590
These efforts not only ensure
that we are learning lessons

01:29:16.590 --> 01:29:19.020
from around the world on these issues,

01:29:19.020 --> 01:29:22.410
but also they also help
us to raise a profile

01:29:22.410 --> 01:29:26.212
of the NHS globally as
well, ensuring that,

01:29:26.212 --> 01:29:30.630
we are a major player in terms
of global knowledge exchange

01:29:30.630 --> 01:29:32.913
in this space as we move forward.

01:29:34.350 --> 01:29:36.840
And finally, our role is not
just to highlight the scale

01:29:36.840 --> 01:29:40.200
of the challenge but to
provide solutions as well.

01:29:40.200 --> 01:29:43.440
And that's why we're
focusing on a collaboration

01:29:43.440 --> 01:29:45.930
with the Institute for
Healthcare Improvement

01:29:45.930 --> 01:29:47.505
to develop what will be

01:29:47.505 --> 01:29:51.463
probably a world first anti-racism model

01:29:51.463 --> 01:29:54.000
on QI and QI methodology.

01:29:54.000 --> 01:29:56.850
It's the first for us,
it's the first for the IHI

01:29:56.850 --> 01:30:00.240
and we will be working with
a number of ICS partners

01:30:00.240 --> 01:30:03.720
across the country to
look at how we can do

01:30:03.720 --> 01:30:06.720
some deep dives around a number of areas,

01:30:06.720 --> 01:30:09.540
maternal health, mental
health, sickle cell disease,

01:30:09.540 --> 01:30:11.970
starting with maternal health

01:30:11.970 --> 01:30:15.870
to begin with as we move forward.

01:30:15.870 --> 01:30:19.140
The final point I'd like to
make is around the relationship

01:30:19.140 --> 01:30:21.810
between engaged and supported staff

01:30:21.810 --> 01:30:24.090
and better outcomes for patients.

01:30:24.090 --> 01:30:26.850
And we know of that
evidence-based relationship

01:30:26.850 --> 01:30:28.860
and going forward we would want to look

01:30:28.860 --> 01:30:31.650
at that particular
relationship given that 25%

01:30:31.650 --> 01:30:33.390
of all staff across the NHS

01:30:33.390 --> 01:30:35.850
are from an ethnic minority background,

01:30:35.850 --> 01:30:37.650
I'd like to thank Chris and Bola,

01:30:37.650 --> 01:30:40.710
I'd like to thank the policy
leads across NHS England,

01:30:40.710 --> 01:30:42.990
who we work with on a day-to-day basis

01:30:42.990 --> 01:30:45.230
and for the commitment of this board

01:30:45.230 --> 01:30:48.510
to the observatory and the
work of the observatory.

01:30:48.510 --> 01:30:50.340
